talk(574,'1982-11-01','A. A. Beilinson','P001059-P001062','Regular singular D-modules and gluing',r'The actual entry begins on P001060, after the preceding Liouville discussion. Gelfand recalls the Lorentz-group reduction to linear algebra; Beilinson then describes regular singular modules on a punctured disk and gluing across a closed subvariety. The final relation \(vu=N\) is shorthand, not a complete definition. Beilinson’s later paper explicitly says it follows S. I. Gelfand’s 1982 notes \citep[introduction]{beilinson1987glue}.')
item(574,'P001060','remark','Lorentz-group representations reduced to linear algebra',r'Gelfand cites his joint work with Ponomarev, approximately 1968, on indecomposable representations of the Lorentz group.',r'For the real Lie algebra of \(SL(2,\mathbb C)\), the category considered consists of \(\mathfrak{su}(2)\)-locally finite modules with finite multiplicity of each compact type; its indecomposable Harish--Chandra modules admit an explicit classification by linear-algebra data. This does not classify arbitrary Hilbert-space representations without admissibility assumptions.','E',r'\citet[introduction and classification]{gelfandponomarev1968lorentz} is the exact 1968 publication recalled in the intervention.',['gelfandponomarev1968lorentz'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The Harish--Chandra and finite-multiplicity conditions are supplied from the cited paper.')
item(574,'P001060','method','The flag-line model for SL(2,R)',r'For \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\), classify \(D\)-modules on \(\mathbb P^1\) with singularities at two fixed points.',r'The complexified maximal compact torus of \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) has two fixed points and one open orbit on the complex flag variety \(\mathbb P^1\). Regular holonomic twisted \(D\)-modules constructible for this orbit stratification give the geometric model, after imposing an infinitesimal character and equivariance appropriate to the representation category.','E',r'The localization framework predates the talk \citep{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation}; the notebook does not specify a block or the precise equivariance.',['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation'],reconstruction='The torus orbit stratification and the conditions for applying localization are editorial specifications, not a claim that all SL(2,R) representations equal all two-singularity modules.',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium')
item(574,'P001060','theorem','A regular singularity as two linear maps',r'On a disk, the category is described by \(\Psi\mathrel{\mathop{\rightleftarrows}^{u}_{v}}\Phi\) with \(uv-\mathrm{Id}\) invertible; outside zero there is a bundle with connection.',r'For a small complex disk \(\Delta\), regular holonomic \(D_\Delta\)-modules whose singular support is contained in the zero section and \(T^*_0\Delta\) correspond, via Riemann--Hilbert, to finite-dimensional vector spaces \(\Psi,\Phi\) and maps \(u:\Psi\to\Phi\), \(v:\Phi\to\Psi\) for which \(1-vu\), equivalently \(1-uv\), is invertible. Morphisms commute with both maps.','A',r'The disk equivalence is \citet[Corollary 3.2]{beilinson1987glue}, written from 1982 notes and published in 1987.',['beilinson1987glue'],reconstruction='Finite-dimensionality, holonomicity and the choice of Riemann--Hilbert convention have been made explicit; the sign is consistent with Beilinson’s convention.')
item(574,'P001060','construction','Ordinary sheaf gluing across a closed subset',r'For \(i:Y\hookrightarrow X\), \(j:U=X\setminus Y\hookrightarrow X\), write \(\mathcal F_X=(\mathcal F_U,\mathcal F_Y,\mathcal F_Y\to i^*j_*\mathcal F_U)\).',r'For sheaves of vector spaces on a topological space, restriction to an open complement and its closed subset, together with the indicated specialization map, determines the sheaf by a fiber-product gluing construction. The analogous assertion for perverse sheaves cannot be obtained by using the same abelian restriction functors unchanged.','E',r'This standard recollement is the motivating elementary comparison for \citet[section 3]{beilinson1987glue}.',['beilinson1987glue'],reconstruction='The abelian sheaf category and the distinction from perverse gluing are specified editorially.',match='partial')
item(574,'P001060','theorem','Nearby and vanishing cycles provide gluing data',r'The final tuple consists of \(M_U,M_Y\), maps to and from \(\Psi(M_U)\), and \(vu=N\), followed by \ru{что-то} (something).',r'For a regular function \(f:X\to\mathbb A^1\), \(Y=f^{-1}(0)\), \(U=X\setminus Y\), perverse sheaves are equivalent to the category of Beilinson gluing data: a perverse sheaf on \(U\), one on \(Y\), and two maps involving unipotent nearby cycles whose composite is its monodromy operator \(N\), with the Tate twist in the algebraic formulation.','A',r'\citet[section 3 and Proposition 3.1]{beilinson1987glue} supplies the precise equivalence; its 1982 circulation is explicit.',['beilinson1987glue'],reconstruction='The function f, unipotent nearby-cycle convention and the meaning of N are supplied from the later text; the notebook alone does not define them.',match='partial')
correction('P001060','Final gluing theorem',r'\(vu=N\), \ru{что-то}.',r'Retain the uncertainty; do not replace \(N\) silently by a fully specified monodromy logarithm.',r'Scan inspected. The word \ru{что-то} is present. The precise operator depends on the nearby-cycle convention; see \citet[section 3]{beilinson1987glue}.',typ='ambiguity')
save()
talk(575,'1982-11-15','I. M. Gelfand','P001060-P001062','Continuous-dimensional Grassmannians and a Schubert analogy',r'The intervention occupies the top of P001061. Gelfand recalls finite Grassmannian cells, then asks about fixed-trace projections in a type \(\mathrm{II}_1\) factor, continuous flags, monotone parameters, topology and cohomology. The notebook expressly calls the problem imprecise, so these questions are preserved as a programme rather than assigned a fabricated solved/open theorem.')
item(575,'P001061','theorem','Schubert cells and Grassmannian cohomology',r'For \(G_{n,k}\), a cell is determined by \(d_j=\dim(V\cap F_j)\); its dimension is written \(\sum d_j\).',r'For a complete flag in \(\mathbb C^n\), let \(1\le a_1<\cdots<a_k\le n\) be the jump positions of \(d_j\). The corresponding Schubert cell is \(\mathbb C^{\sum_i(a_i-i)}\). The even real dimensional cells give a free integral cohomology basis by the dual Schubert classes. The written sum of the intersection dimensions is not this cell dimension.','E',r'The classical cell formula is reviewed in \citet[section 47.2, Exercise 47.4 and Corollary 47.7]{etingof2024lie}; it also occurred as 1971-239.1--2 in the supplied index.',['etingof2024lie'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The field, jump convention and correct dimension are supplied; the notebook’s formula is retained in the correction ledger.')
item(575,'P001061','definition','Fixed-trace projections as continuous Grassmannians',r'In a type \(\mathrm{II}_1\) factor, consider \(\alpha\)-dimensional projections.',r'For a type \(\mathrm{II}_1\) factor \((M,\tau)\) with normalized trace and \(0\le\alpha\le1\), define \(\mathrm{Gr}_\alpha(M)=\{p\in M:p=p^*=p^2,\ \tau(p)=\alpha\}\). Projections of the same trace are unitarily equivalent. The definition does not itself choose a topology on this set.','E',r'Continuous projection dimension is classical von Neumann geometry \citep{vonneumann1960continuous}; earlier factor background occurs at 1969-179.8--10.',['vonneumann1960continuous'],reconstruction='A normalized finite trace and the projection equation are made explicit.',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial')
item(575,'P001061','question','Schubert-type strata for a continuous flag',r'\ru{Есть ли аналог клетки Шуберта?} A continuous flag is indexed by \([0,1]\); the proposed description uses monotone functions.',r'Given a continuous increasing projection flag \((e_t)_{0\le t\le1}\) with \(\tau(e_t)=t\), one candidate invariant of \(p\in\mathrm{Gr}_\alpha(M)\) is \(t\mapsto\tau(p\wedge e_t)\). The notebook does not choose an acting group or say whether equal functions should define an orbit, a cell, or a closure class; hence no classification theorem is determined.','F',r'The continuous-geometry background is identifiable \citep{vonneumann1960continuous}; the exact proposed Schubert equivalence relation is not.',['vonneumann1960continuous'],reconstruction='The meet-trace function is a natural reconstruction of the monotonicity hint, not a uniquely recoverable formula.',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
item(575,'P001061','question','Topology and cohomology of the proposed continuous Grassmannian',r'\ru{какая топология? и какие когомологии?} follows \ru{Непр. когомологии!?}.',r'The question asks for a topology and a cohomology theory on fixed-trace projection spaces compatible with the continuous-flag analogy. Norm, strong-operator and trace-metric topologies lead to different questions; none is selected in the source.','F',r'No unique statement can be matched to a publication until the missing choices are made; continuous geometry alone does not supply the requested cohomology.',['vonneumann1960continuous'],literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='this is an underdetermined historical programme, not evidence that every precise topology/cohomology variant is open.')
correction('P001061','Schubert-cell dimension',r'\(\dim\text{cell}=\sum_jd_j\), where \(d_j=\dim(V\cap F_j)\).',r'For jumps \(a_i\), the cell dimension is \(\sum_i(a_i-i)\).',r'The scan and both transcriptions agree on the written sum. For the zero-dimensional cell \(V=F_k\), \(d_j=\min(j,k)\) has positive sum. This is a faithful mathematical error, not a translation correction.')
save()
