ref('feiginfuchs1982invariant',author='Feigin, B. L. and Fuchs, D. B.',title='Invariant skew-symmetric differential operators on the line and {Verma} modules over the {Virasoro} algebra',journal='Functional Analysis and Its Applications',year=1982,volume=16,number=2,pages='114--126',doi='10.1007/BF01081626',url='https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/faa1619',annote='Math-Net metadata and full 17-page Russian primary paper read, sections 3--4 on Verma modules, contravariant form and determinant. Original Russian 16(2), 47--63. This paper is not cited as a printed bosonic oscillator formula.')
talk(577,'1982-11-22','A. M. Polyakov','P001063-P001071','Liouville uniformization, accessory parameters and Virasoro blocks',r'The talk occupies P001064--P001069. It moves from the Liouville action and cusp uniformization to the stress tensor, accessory parameters, and the proposed recovery of classical geometry from quantum Ward identities. The last pages give a Verma-module expansion and an oscillator realization. The classical generating-function conjecture was proved by Zograf and Takhtajan; the conformal-block programme appears in BPZ 1984, while rigorous sphere Liouville construction and bootstrap came much later. Several factors, powers and completeness relations in the notebook are inconsistent and are explicitly corrected below.')
item(577,'P001064','construction','Liouville action from the string conformal factor',r'In isothermal coordinates \(g_{ab}=e^\varphi\delta_{ab}\), \(S=\gamma^{-1}\int(\tfrac12|\nabla\varphi|^2+e^\varphi)\), with \(\gamma^{-1}=(26-D)/(48\pi)\).',r'The displayed expression is the formal conformal-gauge effective action of the bosonic string, with a choice of cosmological constant absorbed into \(\varphi\). It is an anomaly calculation with regularization and gauge fixing, not a definition of an infinite-dimensional Lebesgue measure. The coefficient is the notebook’s classical normalization.','E',r'Polyakov’s 1981 construction is explicitly identified as the starting point by \citet[introduction]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville}. Their rigorous formulation also explains the quantum background-charge shift.',['davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'],match='partial',reconstruction='The words formal, effective, and the normalization qualification separate the physics derivation from a rigorous measure construction.',status='the conformal anomaly construction is established physics; a rigorous subcritical sphere theory exists, without thereby solving the full bosonic string problem.')
item(577,'P001064','claim','Conformal covariance and the purported unique local action',r'\(e^{\widetilde\varphi}=|w^{\prime}|^2e^{\varphi\circ w}\); the Liouville Lagrangian is called the only invariant local Lagrangian.',r'The stated transformation is the pullback law for a conformal metric, for locally univalent holomorphic \(w\). The kinetic-plus-exponential action has the appropriate covariance up to boundary and background terms. Without a derivative-order restriction the uniqueness claim is false: integrals of local scalar curvature invariants supply additional actions.','E',r'The correctly qualified covariance is part of the classical and quantum formulations \citep[section~1]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville}; \citep[Theorem~3.11]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville}.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville','davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='No uniqueness theorem is asserted without a fixed order, boundary convention and admissible class of Lagrangians.',match='partial')
item(577,'P001064-P001065','theorem','Liouville equation and the developing map',r'\(\Delta\varphi=e^\varphi\), with \(e^\varphi=|w^{\prime}|^2/(\operatorname{Im}w)^2\).',r'For the Euclidean \(\Delta=\partial_x^2+\partial_y^2\), a locally univalent holomorphic map into the upper half-plane gives \(e^\varphi=2|w^{\prime}|^2/(\operatorname{Im}w)^2\) satisfying \(\Delta\varphi=e^\varphi\). Without the factor 2 it satisfies \(\Delta\varphi=2e^\varphi\). The corresponding curvatures are respectively \(-1/2\) and \(-1\).','E',r'This is classical uniformization geometry, reviewed explicitly as old material by \citet[section~1]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville}.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The Laplacian convention is fixed to exhibit the notebook’s factor mismatch; w must have nonzero derivative and nonzero imaginary part.')
item(577,'P001065','construction','Complete cusp metrics on punctured spheres',r'A sphere with \(N\) horns has \(e^\varphi\sim2/(|z-z_i|^2\log^2|z-z_i|)\).',r'For \(N\ge3\) distinct punctures on \(\mathbb P^1\), there is a unique complete finite-area metric of curvature \(-1/2\) in its conformal class. In a puncture coordinate it has the displayed leading cusp asymptotic. Three points may be fixed at \(0,1,\infty\).','E',r'The classical complete hyperbolic setting and puncture asymptotics are reviewed in \citet[section~1]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville}.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],reconstruction='The number of punctures, completeness and curvature normalization are supplied explicitly.')
item(577,'P001065-P001067','method','Quantum correlations and the classical saddle',r'\(\Psi=\int\mathcal D\varphi e^{-S}\sim e^{-S_{cl}}\) as \(\gamma\to0\); later lines write this as an equality involving \(\Delta S_{cl}\).',r'The first formula is a formal saddle-point prescription with singular insertions and a regularized action. The later equality is only a schematic semiclassical statement: quantum correlations include fluctuation terms, and a scaling of insertion charges must be specified.','B',r'\citet[Theorem~3.2]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville}, first circulated in 2014, constructs sphere correlations for coupling \(0<\gamma<2\), \(Q=2/\gamma+\gamma/2\), charges \(\alpha_i<Q\) and \(\sum\alpha_i>2Q\). It supplies a rigorous framework, not the unqualified exact saddle identity.',['davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The probabilistic parameter gamma is a modern convention and is not equated numerically with the notebook’s coupling.',status='rigorous subcritical sphere correlations exist; the notebook’s unscaled equality with a classical exponential is not an exact quantum formula.')
item(577,'P001065','claim','Stress tensor and Schwarzian normalization',r'\(T=\tfrac12\varphi_z^2-\varphi_{zz}\) is holomorphic and is identified with a displayed Schwarzian having denominators \((w^{\prime})^2,(w^{\prime})^4\).',r'On a Liouville solution, \(\partial_{\bar z}T=0\). With the preceding developing map, \(T=-\{w,z\}\), where \(\{w,z\}=w^{\prime\prime\prime}/w^{\prime}-\tfrac32(w^{\prime\prime}/w^{\prime})^2\). Thus the written Schwarzian denominators and the sign identification are inconsistent.','E',r'The projective connection normalization is checked in \citet[section~1]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville}.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='A new symbol q below separates the ODE coefficient from this stress tensor.')
item(577,'P001065','construction','Fuchsian equation and the accessory parameters',r'\(T=\tfrac14\sum(z-z_i)^{-2}+\sum c_i(z-z_i)^{-1}\), \(\psi^{\prime\prime}+T\psi=0\), \(w=\psi_1/\psi_2\); the \(c_i\) are said to be real.',r'Writing \(q=\tfrac12\{w,z\}\), the ratio of independent solutions of \(\psi^{\prime\prime}+q\psi=0\) is a developing map. Cusp singularities give double-pole coefficient \(1/4\); the simple-pole coefficients are accessory parameters, subject to the conditions at infinity. For arbitrary complex punctures they need not be real. Fuchsian uniformization selects monodromy conjugate into \(PSL(2,\mathbb R)\) with the appropriate developing-map geometry.','E',r'\citet[section~1.1]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville} supplies the classical formulation. Real monodromy alone, without the uniformizing branch and geometry, should not be read as a uniqueness assertion for arbitrary projective structures.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='q equals minus one-half of the stress tensor in the preceding item; the notebook uses T for both.')
item(577,'P001065','remark','Varying punctures while preserving Fuchsian reality',r'When the punctures vary, monodromy changes but remains real, in \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\).',r'The family of uniformizing projective connections over punctured-sphere moduli has Fuchsian monodromy. This condition allows the conjugacy class of the representation to vary. It is therefore different from an isomonodromic deformation with a fixed monodromy representation.','E',r'The deformation setting is made precise in \citet[sections~1--2]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville}.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The projective monodromy and marking convention are supplied.')
pid=item(577,'P001066-P001068','conjecture','The Liouville action generates accessory parameters',r'The central claim is \(c_i=\partial S_{cl}/\partial z_i\), to be obtained through quantum theory and the classical limit.',r'On the configuration space of \(N\ge4\) distinct punctures with three fixed, the suitably regularized classical Liouville action generates the accessory parameters. In the Zograf--Takhtajan convention for the full Schwarzian projective connection, \(c_i=-(2\pi)^{-1}\partial S/\partial z_i\). The ODE coefficient q uses half these coefficients.','B',r'Proved in \citet{zograftakhtajan1985action} and in detail in \citet[section~3, Theorem~1]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville}: announcement in 1985, Russian full paper in 1987, English in 1988. The latter introduction explicitly identifies Polyakov’s conjecture.',['zograftakhtajan1985action','zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],problem='GP-1982-21',reconstruction='The regularization, fixed punctures, sign and constant are indispensable; the notebook’s S and T normalizations do not determine them consistently.',status='the punctured-sphere generating-function conjecture is proved in the stated normalization.')
P.append(dict(id='GP-1982-21',title='Polyakov accessory-parameter conjecture',statement=r'For the complete hyperbolic metric on a sphere with distinct punctures, does a suitably regularized classical Liouville action generate the uniformizing accessory parameters under motion of the punctures?',occurrences=[pid],status=r'As of November 2026: proved by Zograf and Takhtajan; 1985 announcement, 1987 Russian full paper, 1988 English translation. With their projective-connection normalization, \(c_i=-(2\pi)^{-1}\partial_{z_i}S\).',status_date='2026-11',refs=['zograftakhtajan1985action','zograftakhtajan1988liouville']))
item(577,'P001066','claim','Canonical momentum in the chiral calculation',r'For the kinetic product \(\varphi_z\varphi_{\bar z}\), the derivative with respect to \(\varphi_{\bar z}\) is written \(p=\varphi_{\bar z}\), followed by a canonical bracket.',r'Treating the two derivatives as independent formal variables gives \(\partial\mathcal L/\partial\varphi_{\bar z}=\varphi_z\), with the action’s overall scale. A genuine equal-time Poisson bracket also requires a real slice, time variable and symplectic form; the two-dimensional complex-coordinate shorthand does not specify these.','E',r'The algebraic differentiation is elementary; the intended stress-tensor algebra has the established conformal-field-theory form \citep[section~2]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='Only the formal derivative is corrected; a canonical structure absent from the notes is not invented.')
item(577,'P001066','theorem','The Virasoro cocycle and Laurent modes',r'The stress bracket has a third derivative of delta. The mode formula omits \(\delta_{n+m,0}\), and the Laurent series omits conformal weight 2.',r'With \(T(z)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}L_nz^{-n-2}\), the modes satisfy \([L_n,L_m]=(n-m)L_{n+m}+\tfrac{c}{12}(n^3-n)\delta_{n+m,0}\). The linear term in n depends on the chosen zero-mode shift. The third-derivative distribution is the local representative of the central cocycle.','E',r'The cohomology predates the lecture \citep{gelfandfuchs1968circle}; the exact mode convention is printed in \citet[section~2, equations~2.20--2.21]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}.',['gelfandfuchs1968circle','belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written')
item(577,'P001066','claim','Anomaly coefficient versus quantum central charge',r'The notebook appends \(c=26-D\) to the Virasoro bracket.',r'For a consistent bosonic string, the matter, Liouville and ghost central charges must sum to zero, giving \(c_L=26-c_{matter}\). In quantum Liouville theory \(c_L=1+6Q^2\). This is not obtained by identifying an unrenormalized classical stress coefficient with the final quantum central charge without specifying the background-charge and field normalization.','B',r'\citet[Theorem~3.11]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville} proves the Weyl anomaly in the rigorous subcritical sphere construction. The interpretation of 26 originates in the pre-talk string calculation.',['davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'],match='partial',reconstruction='This separates the anomaly-balance claim from the quantum renormalization not recorded in the notebook.',status='the indicated anomaly formula is established for the specified Liouville construction; no unrestricted real-coupling construction for arbitrary D is inferred.')
item(577,'P001066-P001069','definition','Primary fields and Ward operators',r'\(\delta_\varepsilon\psi=\varepsilon\psi^{\prime}+\Delta\varepsilon^{\prime}\psi\), but the mode relation repeatedly lacks \(z^n\) in the weight term.',r'A chiral primary of weight h obeys \([L_n,\psi(z)]=(z^{n+1}\partial_z+(n+1)hz^n)\psi(z)\). The local generator is the contour integral \((2\pi i)^{-1}\oint\varepsilon(z)T(z)\,dz\), not the unqualified area integral printed in the notes.','A',r'The primary-field formalism is part of \citet[sections~2--3]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}, received November 1983 and published in 1984.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The weight is denoted h to distinguish it from the Laplacian and the notebook’s other uses of Delta.')
item(577,'P001067-P001068','construction','Highest-weight modules and the four-point cross-ratio',r'Normalize four points to \(\infty,1,x,0\); \(|0\rangle=\psi(0)|v\rangle\) has \(L_n|0\rangle=0\) for \(n>0\), \(L_0|0\rangle=\Delta|0\rangle\).',r'Möbius covariance reduces a sphere four-point function to a function of the cross-ratio and its complex conjugate, times known coordinate factors. A chiral intermediate channel is modeled by a highest-weight module; its universal version is a Verma module. An actual irreducible theory may quotient null states and may require several or continuously many channels.','A',r'\citet[sections~3--4]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} develops this algebraic framework.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],reconstruction='The distinction between a chiral block and a full correlator is essential; the notebook calls both Psi.')
item(577,'P001067-P001068','method','Descendant expansion requires the inverse Gram matrix',r'\(I=\sum|n\rangle\langle n|\), later \(|n\rangle=L_{-n}|0\rangle\); the next line acknowledges nonorthogonality.',r'At level N, descendants are indexed by all partitions \(\lambda\vdash N\), not just one vector. If their contravariant Gram matrix \(G_N\) is invertible, the level projector is \(\sum_{\lambda,\mu}|\lambda\rangle(G_N^{-1})_{\lambda\mu}\langle\mu|\). At degenerate parameters pass to a suitable quotient or formulate a meromorphic block.','A',r'The block machinery is explicit in \citet[section~4]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}; the contravariant-form and singular-vector structure was already treated in \citet[section~3]{feiginfuchs1982invariant}.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite','feiginfuchs1982invariant'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='This is the missing linear-algebra operation, not a claim that the author overlooked the problem; the notes explicitly register it.')
item(577,'P001067','claim','The first equal-weight block coefficient',r'The commutator gives \(\langle1|\psi(x)|0\rangle=\Delta x^{1-\Delta}\), followed by \(x^{-\Delta}(1+\Delta x/2+\cdots)\).',r'For a chiral block whose four external weights and intermediate weight all equal h, with normalized primary three-point factors, the level-one contribution is \(h^2/(2h)=h/2\) when h is nonzero. Thus the displayed coefficient is correct after inserting the missing inverse norm \(\langle L_{-1}h,L_{-1}h\rangle=2h\).','A',r'The general block expansion is \citet[section~4]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}; the level-one verification here follows directly from the stated Virasoro and Ward relations.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The equal internal weight and normalization explain why this special coefficient is not a universal four-point coefficient.')
item(577,'P001067','question','Coalescing punctures and accessory coefficients',r'As \(z_i-z_j\to0\), does \(c_i\) go over to the preceding accessory coefficient?',r'A precise degeneration question must say which punctures remain, whether the colliding coefficient or a surviving one is examined, and what divergent principal part is subtracted. The source does not make these choices. Colliding cusp points cannot simply be substituted into the distinct-puncture formula.','F',r'\citet[section~3, Remark~7]{zograftakhtajan1988liouville} discusses degeneration as a further application, but is not itself the unqualified limit claimed here. No single modern open problem is inferred from the abbreviated question.',['zograftakhtajan1988liouville'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',status='the source’s unspecified limit cannot receive a unique solved/open status.')
item(577,'P001068','construction','Bosonic oscillators realize the Virasoro algebra',r'For positive n the notes write \(L_n=(\alpha+i\beta n)a_n+\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}a_ka_{n-k}+\sum_{k\ge1}a_k^+a_{n+k}\), with \([a_n,a_m^+]=n\delta_{nm}\).',r'The first quadratic sum requires a factor \(1/2\). With real \(\alpha,\beta\), \(L_{-n}=L_n^*\), \(L_0=\sum_{k\ge1}a_k^+a_k+h\), \(h=(\alpha^2+\beta^2)/2\), the corrected operators on finite-particle vectors have central charge \(c=1+12\beta^2\).','E',r'\citet[Theorem~2 and its preceding bosonic construction]{neretin1983highest} prints the associated group realization and attributes the Lie-algebra construction to Chodos--Thorn (1974). The pre-talk attribution is indirect through this primary 1983 paper.',['neretin1983highest'],literal='false as written',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r'The factor \(1/2\) and \(L_0\) are added to make the formula internally consistent. A vacuum level-two check is recorded in checks.py.')
item(577,'P001068','claim','Oscillator and Verma bases agree only generically',r'The vectors \(a_{n_1}^+a_{n_2}^+\cdots|0\rangle\) and \(L_{-n_1}L_{-n_2}\cdots|0\rangle\) are said to be mutually expressible; the former are orthogonal.',r'Normalized occupation-number vectors form an orthogonal Fock basis. The change from Virasoro descendants is invertible at a fixed level only when the corresponding transition matrix has nonzero determinant. Singular vectors and special parameters can make the Verma-to-Fock map fail to be an isomorphism.','E',r'The need to separate generic and degenerate highest-weight parameters is established in \citet[section~3]{feiginfuchs1982invariant}; \citet[Theorem~2]{neretin1983highest} gives the Fock realization.',['feiginfuchs1982invariant','neretin1983highest'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(577,'P001068','method','Quadratic current and the Miura-type stress tensor',r'\(T=p^{\prime}+p^2\), with a formal mode expansion of p.',r'The classical relation expresses a projective connection as a quadratic current plus a derivative. In the quantum oscillator realization the quadratic product must be normally ordered, and its derivative coefficient, zero mode and Laurent powers must match the chosen central charge. The written formula is schematic, not the complete normal-ordered operator identity.','E',r'The corrected realization is checked through \citet[Theorem~2]{neretin1983highest}; it belongs to the pre-existing bosonic construction recalled there.',['neretin1983highest'],match='partial',reconstruction='No numerical coefficient is forced from the notebook’s unnormalized p.')
item(577,'P001069','method','Bargmann coordinates turn Ward identities into PDEs',r'Put \(a_n=\partial_{z_n}\), \(a_n^+=nz_n\), then write a differential equation for \(F(x,z_1,z_2,\ldots)\).',r'On polynomials in oscillator coordinates these operators satisfy the stated Heisenberg relations. Substitution into the corrected \(L_n\) gives \([(\alpha+i\beta n)\partial_{z_n}+\tfrac12\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\partial_{z_k}\partial_{z_{n-k}}+\sum_{k\ge1}kz_k\partial_{z_{n+k}}]F=(x^{n+1}\partial_x+(n+1)hx^n)F\), provided F is the corresponding highest-weight matrix element.','A',r'This is an explicit combination of the oscillator and Ward constructions subsequently treated in \citet{neretin1983highest} and \citet[sections~3--4]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}. It is a system of constraints; uniqueness needs initial and representation data.',['neretin1983highest','belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The factors \(1/2\) and \(x^n\) are emendations; the function space and highest-weight input are editorial qualifications.')
# Related rigorous outcome, attached to the mathematical claim actually proposed rather than a new problem.
item(577,'P001067-P001069','claim','Reconstructing four-point correlations from conformal blocks',r'The notes propose computing the coefficients algebraically and thereby determining \(\Psi\).',r'Ward identities determine descendants within a specified channel. A full four-point function additionally needs primary spectrum, three-point constants, a pairing of chiral and antichiral blocks, and an integration or summation prescription. The notebook does not supply those data.','B',r'\citet[Theorem~1.1]{guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap}, first circulated in 2020, proves the Liouville four-point bootstrap for \(0<\gamma<2\), \(\alpha_i<Q\), \(\alpha_1+\alpha_2>Q\), \(\alpha_3+\alpha_4>Q\), using the spectral integral and structure constants. This is a precise later realization, not a proof that symmetry alone specifies any CFT.',['guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap'],match='partial',reconstruction='The modern theorem states a definite real-coupling range and channel conditions absent from the notes.',status='the specified Liouville bootstrap theorem is proved; the statement is not enlarged to all parameter ranges or all conformal theories.')
correction('P001064','Uniqueness of the local invariant Lagrangian','The only local invariant Lagrangian.',r'Qualify by admissible derivative order, total divergences and background terms; unrestricted uniqueness is false.',r'The scan says local without an order bound. For example a curvature-squared metric action provides a higher-derivative local invariant.')
correction('P001065','Equations 5--6',r'\(\Delta\varphi=e^\varphi\), \(e^\varphi=|w^\prime|^2/(\operatorname{Im}w)^2\).',r'For Euclidean Delta insert 2 in the numerator, or replace the equation by \(\Delta\varphi=2e^\varphi\).',r'Both scans agree; \(w(z)=z\) gives \(\Delta(-2\log y)=2/y^2\). The cusp formula on the same page already contains 2.')
correction('P001065','Equations 9--12',r'Schwarzian denominators \((w^\prime)^2,(w^\prime)^4\) and a common symbol T.',r'\(\{w,z\}=w^{\prime\prime\prime}/w^\prime-\tfrac32(w^{\prime\prime}/w^\prime)^2\); the stress tensor is minus this, while the ODE coefficient is half this.',r'Scan confirms the powers. Direct differentiation and the ratio-of-solutions Schwarzian identity independently fix the normalizations.')
correction('P001065','Reality of accessory parameters','The accessory coefficients must be real.',r'The uniformizing monodromy is conjugate into PSL(2,R); coefficients need not be real for arbitrary complex punctures.',r'Russian and English agree; the surrounding text does not restrict punctures to the real axis. The classical projective-connection formula makes the distinction.')
correction('P001065','Solutions following equation~13','Two independent solutions of 13.','Two independent solutions of 12.',r'Equation 12 is the second-order ODE; 13 is their quotient. Scan confirms the self-reference.')
correction('P001066','Equation 16',r'\(\partial\mathcal L/\partial\varphi_{\bar z}=\varphi_{\bar z}\).',r'\(\partial\mathcal L/\partial\varphi_{\bar z}=\varphi_z\), with the overall normalization.',r'The separate bar on the final z is visible in the scan; elementary differentiation of the preceding kinetic product reverses it.')
correction('P001066','Equations 18--19','Laurent modes without weight 2 and central term without Kronecker delta.',r'Use \(T(z)=\sum L_nz^{-n-2}\) and central term \(c(n^3-n)\delta_{n+m,0}/12\).',r'The scan contains no delta. Antisymmetry already fails without it, for example \(n=m=2\); BPZ section 2 confirms the conventional modes.')
correction('P001066','Equation 20',r'\(T_\varepsilon=\int\varepsilon T\,d^2z\).',r'For the holomorphic charge use \((2\pi i)^{-1}\oint\varepsilon T\,dz\).',r'Scan says d²z; a holomorphic mode generator is a contour charge. Other real-time conventions require an explicitly specified slice.')
for pg,pl in [('P001066','Equation 22'),('P001068','Primary-field axiom'),('P001069','Right-hand Ward operator')]:
 correction(pg,pl,r'\((n+1)\Delta\psi\) or \((n+1)\Delta F\).',r'Insert \(z^n\), or \(x^n\) in the x-coordinate version.',r'All three scan lines omit the factor; substituting \(\epsilon=z^{n+1}\) into the immediately preceding primary variation requires it.')
correction('P001067-P001068','Resolution of the identity',r'\(I=\sum|n\rangle\langle n|\), \(|n\rangle=L_{-n}|0\rangle\).',r'Sum over all partition descendants with the inverse Gram matrix; restrict or quotient when degenerate.',r'The source itself notes nonorthogonality. At level 2 there are two descendants and at level 1 the norm is \(2\Delta\), so the displayed resolution is not literal.')
correction('P001068-P001069','Bosonic quadratic sum',r'\(\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}a_ka_{n-k}\) and the corresponding second-derivative sum.',r'Insert a factor \(1/2\).',r'Scan confirms its absence. At \(\alpha=\beta=0\), \(n=2\), the vacuum commutator gives 2 instead of the required \(c/2=1/2\); inserting \(1/2\) gives the correct value.')
