talk(580,'1983-02-07','A. A. Kirillov','P001074-P001075','Near-unitary circle actions and Fock-space representations',r'The record occupies the upper part of P001074. Following Gelfand’s methodological and supersymmetry remarks, Kirillov reports Neretin’s weighted pullbacks, their Hilbert--Schmidt failure of unitarity, and Gaussian/Fock unitarization. This construction had already appeared in 1982; extending the action on the original one-particle space and constructing a new unitary action on Fock space must be distinguished \citep{neretin1982complementary}.')
item(580,'P001074','remark','A call for infinite-dimensional symplectic geometry',r'Gelfand says that infinite-dimensional symplectic geometry is not yet familiar and calls Fock space a toy for mathematicians.',r'The intervention requests a geometric understanding beyond formal oscillator manipulations. It specifies neither a category of infinite-dimensional manifolds nor a theorem to prove.','F',r'The restricted Hilbert-space groups used immediately afterwards are identifiable in \citet[section 1]{neretin1982complementary}; they do not uniquely specify the broader programme.',['neretin1982complementary'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(580,'P001074','method','Supersymmetric derivation of fixed point formulas',r'Popular lectures by Witten are mentioned, with an arrow to the Atiyah--Bott fixed point theorem.',r'In supersymmetric quantum mechanics on a compact Riemannian manifold, a deformation of the de Rham/signature complex by a Killing field localizes an index calculation at its zeros. For isolated zeros this yields the corresponding fixed point contributions. The note does not identify a particular elliptic complex or a unique preprint.','E',r'\citet[section 3, equations (44)--(45)]{witten1982supersymmetry} supplies this calculation in a 1982 paper, received 26 March 1982. The mathematical identification is stronger than the bibliographic identification of the unnamed lectures.',['witten1982supersymmetry'],reconstruction='Compactness, the Killing-field setting and the chosen complexes are supplied from the source.',match='partial',confidence='medium',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(580,'P001074','construction','Weighted pullback extends the Möbius action',r'A fractional-linear action with a factor \(|\beta z+\delta|^\lambda\) is extended to diffeomorphisms by \(f\mapsto(f\circ\varphi^{-1})|D\varphi^{-1}|^{-\lambda/2}\).',r'For any real density weight a, \(T_a(\varphi)f=(f\circ\varphi^{-1})|(\varphi^{-1})^{\prime}|^a\) defines a representation of orientation-preserving smooth circle diffeomorphisms on smooth densities in a local trivialization. Its restriction to Möbius transformations has the familiar multiplier. Unitarity depends on the Hilbert form and parameter; it does not follow from the extension formula.','E',r'The weighted pullback is \citet[section 2, equation (2)]{neretin1982complementary}, with a different parameter convention.',['neretin1982complementary'],reconstruction='A neutral weight a avoids identifying the notebook’s lambda with the publication’s lambda without the required reparametrization.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(580,'P001074','theorem','The complementary-series inner product is almost invariant',r'The imaginary part of a double integral with kernel \(|x_1-x_2|^{-2+\lambda}/\Gamma(-1+\lambda)\) is Möbius invariant; under general diffeomorphisms the operator is marked \(U(1+\mathrm{HS})\).',r'For Neretin’s positive complementary-series Hilbert completions, with his parameter \(0<\rho<2\), \(\rho\ne1\), the weighted pullbacks are bounded invertible and have polar form \(O(1+K)\), with K Hilbert--Schmidt. In that range they are orthogonal exactly for Möbius transformations. Analytic continuation and a parameter-dependent normalization are required outside the convergent integral range.','E',r'This is \citet[Theorem 1 and the following proposition]{neretin1982complementary}, already published in 1982.',['neretin1982complementary'],reconstruction='The positivity range is stated in the publication’s parameter, not asserted for every value of the handwritten lambda. The imaginary-part form is on the complexification of the positive real Hilbert space.')
item(580,'P001074','construction','Gaussian unitarization on Fock space',r'The margin corrects the phrase unitarily: the weighted action is nonunitary, but unitary representations are constructed on Fock space using coherent states.',r'If a real Hilbert-space representation takes values in \(GL_0(H)=\{O(1+K):K\text{ Hilbert--Schmidt}\}\), the associated transformations of a Gaussian realization are quasi-invariant. Composition multiplied by the square root of the Radon--Nikodym derivative gives a unitary representation on Gaussian \(L^2\), equivalently the symmetric Fock space. This changes the representation space.','E',r'\citet[section 1 and the construction after Theorem 1]{neretin1982complementary} gives exactly this mechanism. Its restriction to the Möbius subgroup is a Fock representation, not the original single complementary-series representation.',['neretin1982complementary'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
correction('P001074','Kirillov’s phrase extends unitarily',r'The \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) representation extends unitarily to \(\operatorname{Diff}(S^1)\).','The weighted pullback extends algebraically and is generally not unitary; the new Fock/Gaussian representation is unitary.',r'The scan itself inserts \ru{это неунитарное, но} beside the pullback formula. The two representation spaces must not be conflated.',typ='ambiguity')
save()
ref('feigin1984semiinfinite',author='Feigin, B. L.',title='The semi-infinite homology of {Kac--Moody} and {Virasoro} {Lie} algebras',journal='Russian Mathematical Surveys',year=1984,volume=39,number=2,pages='155--156',doi='10.1070/RM1984v039n02ABEH003112',url='https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/rm2354',annote='Complete English primary PDF read. Constructions 1--2, Example 1 theorem and final physical-state paragraph checked. Received 15 January 1983; published 1984. No earlier public preprint date was verified.')
ref('neretin1984padic',author='Neretin, Yu. A.',title='Unitary representations of the diffeomorphism group of the p-adic projective line',journal='Functional Analysis and Its Applications',year=1984,volume=18,number=4,pages='345--346',doi='10.1007/BF01083703',url='https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/faa1505',annote='Math-Net metadata and author-hosted primary scan opened; complete two-page text inspected after local rendering/OCR. Introduction explicitly credits I. M. Gelfand with the p-adic analogue problem. Theorem 1 and Gaussian construction checked. Received 10 January 1984; publication 1984.')
ref('marsdenweinstein1974reduction',author='Marsden, Jerrold E. and Weinstein, Alan',title='Reduction of symplectic manifolds with symmetry',journal='Reports on Mathematical Physics',year=1974,volume=5,number=1,pages='121--130',doi='10.1016/0034-4877(74)90021-4',url='https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/8k5v6-4pa21',annote='Author repository and indexed primary abstract checked. Direct full-PDF retrieval failed; the standard regular-value formulation is an editorial reconstruction, not recorded as a fresh reading of the theorem. Received 15 September 1972; publication February 1974.')
talk(581,'1983-02-07','B. L. Feigin','P001074-P001077','String constraints and semi-infinite homology',r'The actual notes occupy the lower P001074 and P001075. They pass from the loop-space symplectic form to oscillator quantization, explain the central-term obstruction to ordinary invariants, and replace invariants by the image of positive-mode invariants in negative-mode coinvariants. Feigin’s 1984 note identifies this physical-state construction with semi-infinite homology. Gelfand’s closing p-adic request has a separately documented answer in Neretin’s 1984 paper.')
item(581,'P001074','construction','The loop-space symplectic form modulo constants',r'For maps \(f,g:S^1\to\mathbb R^d\), with a Riemannian or Lorentzian target metric, use \(\omega(f,g)=\int_{S^1}(f,dg)\); quotient by constant maps.',r'For a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form b on \(\mathbb R^d\), the form \(\omega(f,g)=\int b(f,g^{\prime})dt\) on smooth loops is skew by integration by parts. Its radical is exactly the constant loops, so it descends to a weak symplectic form on \(C^\infty(S^1,\mathbb R^d)/\mathbb R^d\). Orientation-preserving reparametrizations preserve it. Positivity of b is unnecessary for this algebraic assertion.','E',r'This is the classical oscillator phase space underlying the final construction in \citet{feigin1984semiinfinite}; the scalar endpoint realization is already in \citet[section 4]{neretin1982complementary}.',['feigin1984semiinfinite','neretin1982complementary'],reconstruction='Smoothness, orientation, nondegeneracy of the target metric and weak rather than strong symplectic topology are made explicit.',match='partial')
item(581,'P001074','claim','The quotient carries a Poisson algebra, not automatically a smooth manifold',r'The space \(V^{\prime}/\operatorname{Diff}S^1\) is said to have a Hamiltonian structure.',r'Invariant observables in a symplectic Poisson algebra are closed under the bracket when the action preserves the symplectic structure. This defines a quotient Poisson algebra. A smooth Poisson manifold structure on the orbit space additionally requires a suitable regular quotient; the loop-space quotient in the notebook is generally singular and no such manifold theorem is established.','E',r'The finite-dimensional reduction principle is older \citep{marsdenweinstein1974reduction}. Its infinite-dimensional application here is formal unless a class of admissible observables and a quotient category are chosen.',['marsdenweinstein1974reduction'],reconstruction='The algebra of observables is the precise interpretation supported without additional regularity hypotheses.',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial')
item(581,'P001074','theorem','Symplectic reduction requires a moment-map level',r'Kirillov describes a reduced symplectic manifold and says the invariant Poisson bracket is degenerate and one must take a quotient.',r'For a finite-dimensional Hamiltonian G-action with equivariant moment map \(\mu\), if \(\xi\) is a regular value and \(G_\xi\) acts freely and properly on \(\mu^{-1}(\xi)\), then \(\mu^{-1}(\xi)/G_\xi\) is symplectic, with reduced form characterized by pullback. The full orbit space \(M/G\) is not generally symplectic.','E',r'This is the regular reduction construction of \citet{marsdenweinstein1974reduction}, published in 1974.',['marsdenweinstein1974reduction'],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',reconstruction='The level set, Hamiltonian hypothesis and free proper regularity assumptions are restored editorially.',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial')
item(581,'P001074-P001075','construction','Polarization and the symmetric oscillator space',r'Complexify \(V^{\prime}=H^+\oplus H^-\), using Fourier modes analytic inside and outside the circle; the notes display \(\Phi=\bigoplus_i S^iH_+\).',r'A positive/negative Fourier polarization gives two paired complex Lagrangian spaces. The algebraic bosonic Fock module is a symmetric algebra on the creation space; annihilation acts by contractions with the dual polarization. Which summand is labelled plus is conventional. A Hilbert completion and positive inner product need an additional choice; a Lorentzian target does not provide a positive Hilbert norm by itself.','E',r'The oscillator construction predates the lecture and is recalled in \citet[section 1]{neretin1982complementary}.',['neretin1982complementary'],reconstruction='The duality implicit in the inconsistent plus/creation labels and the distinction between algebraic and Hilbert Fock spaces are stated explicitly.',match='partial')
item(581,'P001074-P001075','claim','The Schwinger term obstructs ordinary invariants',r'The map from vector fields through symplectic transformations to the Weyl representation is projective; a Schwinger term appears and invariants do not exist.',r'The quantized action factors through the Virasoro central extension. In a module on which its central element acts by a nonzero scalar, no nonzero vector is annihilated by every Virasoro generator including the center. For d free scalar oscillators with standard normal ordering, the matter central charge is d, also for a nondegenerate indefinite target form.','E',r'The central extension is older \citep{gelfandfuchs1968circle}; Feigin’s string construction explicitly treats the resulting central charge \citep[Example 1 and final paragraph]{feigin1984semiinfinite}.',['gelfandfuchs1968circle','feigin1984semiinfinite'],reconstruction='The precise invariant obstruction and the normal-ordering convention replace the loose phrase invariants do not exist.',match='partial')
item(581,'P001075','construction','Physical states as an image of invariants in coinvariants',r'For \(L_+=\langle L_1,L_2,\ldots\rangle\), \(L_-=\langle L_{-1},L_{-2},\ldots\rangle\), take the image of \(\Phi^{L_+}\to\Phi/L_-\Phi\), then select an \(L_0\) condition.',r'For the string oscillator module, form \(Q^{\prime}=\operatorname{im}(H^0(L_+,\Phi)\to H_0(L_-,\Phi))\). Physical states require a further zero-mode condition, \(e_0v=-v\) in Feigin’s sign convention. Thus invariants are retained only modulo spurious negative-mode states; they are not the full Virasoro-invariant subspace.','A',r'\citet[final physical-state paragraph]{feigin1984semiinfinite} states this exact construction and identifies it with degree-zero semi-infinite homology. Received 15 January 1983, published in 1984.',['feigin1984semiinfinite'],reconstruction='The zero-mode eigenvalue is supplied from the publication; the notebook’s subscript does not specify it unambiguously.')
item(581,'P001075','theorem','The critical charge 26 in semi-infinite homology',r'The resulting space is nontrivial at least for \(d=26\).',r'For Feigin’s graded category of Virasoro modules, the relative semi-infinite Tor functors of two modules with scalar central charges \(c_1,c_2\) vanish unless \(c_1+c_2=26\). In the string construction the fermionic complex contributes charge \(-26\), balancing the d-boson charge at \(d=26\). This necessary charge balance is not a blanket assertion that every module at charge 26 has nonzero physical states.','A',r'\citet[Example 1, theorem (a), and final paragraph]{feigin1984semiinfinite} gives the precise homological statement.',['feigin1984semiinfinite'],reconstruction='The categorical assumptions and distinction between necessary charge cancellation and arbitrary-module nonvanishing are supplied from the paper.',match='partial')
pid=item(581,'P001075','problem','A p-adic analogue of diffeomorphism-group representations',r'Gelfand asks: \ru{Построить p-адич. аналог предст. группы диффеоморфизмов}.',r'Construct unitary representations for a p-adic analogue of the circle diffeomorphism group by replacing the real-circle almost-invariant Hilbert structure and Fock/Gaussian construction. The subsequently documented realization uses analytic diffeomorphisms of \(\mathbb P^1(\mathbb Q_p)\), embedded in the group of local automorphisms of the Bruhat--Tits tree boundary.','B',r'\citet[introduction and Theorem 1]{neretin1984padic} explicitly attributes this question to Gelfand and constructs the required near-orthogonal action and Gaussian unitary representations. Received 10 January 1984, published 1984.',['neretin1984padic'],reconstruction='The precise p-adic group and Hilbert construction come from the solution, not the one-line question. No p-adic Virasoro highest-weight theorem is claimed.',problem='GP-1982-22',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',match='partial',status='the analytic-projective-line version explicitly associated with Gelfand’s request is solved; other possible meanings of p-adic analogue are not identified with it.')
P.append(dict(id='GP-1982-22',title='A p-adic analogue of the circle-diffeomorphism representations',statement=r'Construct a p-adic version of the almost-invariant-kernel and Fock/Gaussian construction of unitary diffeomorphism-group representations. The documented realization takes analytic diffeomorphisms of \(\mathbb P^1(\mathbb Q_p)\).',occurrences=[pid],status=r'As of November 2026: Neretin’s 1984 construction solves the explicitly credited analytic-projective-line version \citep[introduction and Theorem 1]{neretin1984padic}. The group specification is supplied by that solution.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['neretin1984padic']))
