talk(582,'1983-02-14','Yu. A. Neretin','P001075-P001078','Hilbert transforms, almost invariant kernels and circle representations',r'The entry is P001076--P001077, including a name of Ginzburg crossed out before the final continuation question. The 1982 near-orthogonal construction is refined by endpoint complex structures and restricted symplectic actions, published in Neretin’s 1983 Doklady note. Gelfand’s pseudodifferential generalization remains unspecified. The written logarithmic convolution cannot yield the claimed derivative of a delta distribution; the Fock-space question needs a completed symmetric space rather than arbitrary analytic sequences.')
item(582,'P001076','theorem','The almost invariant kernel needs a positivity qualification',r'For every \(\rho\), a power-kernel inner product is almost preserved, with operator \(O(1+T)\), T Hilbert--Schmidt.',r'In the complementary interval the appropriately normalized and continued power kernel defines a positive Hilbert form and a near-orthogonal smooth diffeomorphism action. Outside it the unmodified form need not be positive. Neretin constructs positive forms for nonintegral parameters by adding a smooth kernel; their almost-invariant equivalence class is independent of that modification.','A',r'The interval case is already \citet[Theorem 1]{neretin1982complementary}; the qualified extension is \citet[Theorems 1--2]{neretin1983boson}, received 20 January 1983 and published later that year.',['neretin1982complementary','neretin1983boson'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The parameter restrictions, analytic continuation and smooth correction are indispensable additions to the phrase for every rho.',match='partial')
item(582,'P001076','construction','The periodic Hilbert transform is a complex structure modulo constants',r'The cotangent integral sends \(\cos nt\mapsto-\sin nt\), \(\sin nt\mapsto\cos nt\), and constants to zero.',r'Interpret the singular integral in the principal-value sense. With the displayed sign convention, H acts on nonzero Fourier modes by \(i\operatorname{sgn}(n)\), and \(H^2=-1\) on mean-zero functions or the quotient by constants. It therefore defines a real complex structure on the appropriate Sobolev completions.','E',r'The classical Hilbert-transform identity is used in \citet[Theorem 3]{neretin1983boson}; only its application to the restricted symplectic representation is new there.',['neretin1983boson'],reconstruction='Principal value, removal of the zero mode and the Fourier multiplier make the formal integral meaningful.',match='partial')
item(582,'P001076','definition','Two endpoint Hilbert forms',r'The endpoint cases are written as a logarithmic kernel and as a cotangent kernel with a derivative; one removes constants or imposes zero mean.',r'Up to positive normalization, the endpoint norms have Fourier expressions \(\sum_{n\ne0}|n|^{-1}|f_n|^2\) on mean-zero density coefficients and \(\sum_{n\ne0}|n||f_n|^2\) on functions modulo constants. The former is represented by the negative logarithmic sine kernel. The unmodified positive logarithm in the notes is negative on every nonzero mean-zero real Fourier mode.','E',r'The endpoint constructions already appear in \citet[section 4]{neretin1982complementary}; \citet[section 2]{neretin1983boson} incorporates them into the common framework.',['neretin1982complementary','neretin1983boson'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='Fourier norms specify a positive convention without silently treating the handwritten logarithm as positive.')
item(582,'P001076-P001077','theorem','Endpoint actions preserve a symplectic form',r'Extend the scalar product using H; its imaginary part is preserved by all diffeomorphisms, and \(\omega(f,g)=\langle f,Hg\rangle\).',r'For either endpoint Hilbert space with its specified density action, the Hilbert transform supplies a compatible complex structure; smooth orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms preserve the associated symplectic form and belong to the restricted symplectic group. On the function realization the form is, up to sign and normalization, \(\int f\,dg\). This stronger exact invariance is an endpoint statement, not a consequence of near-orthogonality at every parameter.','A',r'This is \citet[Theorem 3]{neretin1983boson}; the paper also notes that the polar defect is trace class in its constructions.',['neretin1983boson'],reconstruction='The endpoint restriction and the respective function/density actions are restored.',match='exact')
item(582,'P001077','construction','Normalized Hardy projections and Lagrangian subspaces',r'\(H\pm i\) are called projections, with Lagrangian images.',r'If \(H^2=-1\), the projections onto its \(\pm i\) eigenspaces in the complexification are \(P_\pm=(1\mp iH)/2\). Their images are the positive and negative Fourier polarizations and are Lagrangian for the complex bilinear extension of the symplectic form. The unscaled \(H\pm i\) have those images but are not idempotents.','E',r'This is the linear algebra of the Hilbert-transform structure used in \citet[Theorem 3 and section 3]{neretin1983boson}.',['neretin1983boson'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='Projection normalization and complex bilinear, rather than Hermitian, isotropy are explicit.',match='partial')
item(582,'P001076-P001077','question','A general pseudodifferential invariance principle',r'Gelfand asks for a general statement about pseudodifferential operators invariant under all diffeomorphisms, and for the full symbol series beyond \(c(1+\text{compact})\).',r'The proposed generalization does not specify the manifold dimension, density weights, operator order, topology of the remainder ideal, or whether equality is exact or modulo smoothing operators. These choices distinguish different classification and covariance problems.','F',r'Neretin’s concrete almost-invariant kernels are covered by \citet[section 2]{neretin1983boson}; that result does not resolve every possible pseudodifferential generalization.',['neretin1983boson'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
item(582,'P001077','claim','The logarithmic Hilbert convolution is not a delta derivative',r'The integral \(\int\log|(t-s)/2|\,(s-\sigma)^{-1}ds\) is expected to give \(\delta^{\prime}\), with analytic continuation proposed.',r'After principal-value/tempered regularization, away from zero Fourier frequency the logarithmic kernel has multiplier proportional to \(|\xi|^{-1}\), while the Hilbert kernel has multiplier proportional to \(\operatorname{sgn}\xi\). Their product has order \(-1\), proportional to \(1/\xi\), whereas \(\delta^{\prime}\) has order \(+1\). Thus the displayed convolution cannot equal \(\delta^{\prime}\); at the symbolic level it is an antiderivative-type kernel. Zero-frequency ambiguities do not repair this mismatch.','E',r'The endpoint formulas in \citet[section 2]{neretin1983boson} distinguish the inverse-derivative and derivative norms. The Fourier-order check here identifies a faithful error in the written intermediate calculation.',['neretin1983boson'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='Regularization and exclusion of zero frequency are explicit; no globally defined unregularized convolution is asserted.')
item(582,'P001077','method','Analytic continuation and differentiation of homogeneous kernels',r'Split \(\int|x|^\lambda f(x)dx\) into \(|x|<1\) and its complement, continue analytically, and differentiate in lambda to get a logarithm.',r'For Schwartz test functions the family \(|x|^\lambda\), initially \(\operatorname{Re}\lambda>-1\), extends meromorphically as a tempered distribution by subtracting Taylor coefficients at zero. At regular parameters, differentiation yields \(|x|^\lambda\log|x|\); at poles one must specify finite parts and residue terms. This procedure defines kernels but does not validate the preceding incorrect convolution identity.','E',r'Analytic regularization is already explicitly used in \citet[section 2]{neretin1982complementary} and \citet[section 2]{neretin1983boson}, which cites the earlier Gelfand--Shilov distribution theory.',['neretin1982complementary','neretin1983boson'],reconstruction='The test-function class and meromorphic, rather than everywhere holomorphic, continuation are supplied.',match='partial')
item(582,'P001077','question','Realizing circle diffeomorphisms on analytic symmetric sequences',r'How does a diffeomorphism act on \(\{f_1(z),f_2(z_1,z_1),f_3(z_1,z_2,z_3),\ldots\}\)? For Möbius maps it agrees with the usual action.',r'Choose a positive-frequency one-particle Hilbert space H and its completed symmetric Fock space \(\widehat{\bigoplus}_{n\ge0}\operatorname{Sym}^nH\). Its vectors can be realized as symmetric holomorphic n-variable components satisfying the Fock norm condition. Restricted symplectic implementers act projectively on this completion; for Möbius transformations preserving the polarization this reduces to second quantization of the one-particle action. Arbitrary sequences of holomorphic functions are not automatically vectors of this representation.','A',r'\citet[section 3 and Theorems 4--5]{neretin1983boson} gives the implementation and endpoint highest-weight representations.',['neretin1983boson'],reconstruction='Symmetry, the vacuum component, Hilbert completion and the repeated second argument’s correction are editorial specifications.',match='partial')
item(582,'P001077','question','Dependence on the smoothness of a diffeomorphism',r'The final question asks what effect smoothness has.',r'The notebook does not choose a regularity scale or ask a specific threshold question: extension from smooth diffeomorphisms to Sobolev, Hölder, or weaker maps gives different problems. Neretin’s construction quoted here is continuous for the smooth topology, which does not settle an unspecified optimal regularity threshold.','F',r'\citet[section 1 and final remark in section 3]{neretin1983boson} states the smooth-topology construction. No unique stronger question follows from the notebook.',['neretin1983boson'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
correction('P001076','Power kernel for every rho','For every rho the displayed expression is an inner product.','Restrict to a positive parameter range or add the appropriate smooth correction and regularization.',r'The scan says every rho. Neretin (1983), Theorems 1--2, explicitly distinguishes positive complementary kernels from indefinite kernels and their corrected positive forms.')
correction('P001076','Logarithmic scalar product',r'\(\iint\log|\sin((t-s)/2)|f(t)f(s)dt\,ds\).','Insert an overall minus sign for a positive norm on real mean-zero functions.',r'The scan has a positive logarithm. Its nonzero Fourier coefficients are negative; testing a cosine gives a negative quadratic form. The sign is a mathematical emendation.')
correction('P001077','Projections',r'\(H\pm i\).',r'\(P_\pm=(1\mp iH)/2\), with the same eigenspace images.',r'The scan explicitly has H plus/minus i. Squaring proves these operators are not idempotent; H squared equals minus the identity on the quotient.')
correction('P001077','Expected logarithmic convolution',r'The displayed log/PV(1/x) convolution should give \(\delta^{\prime}\).','The displayed kernels have product Fourier order minus one, not plus one; additional derivatives or inverse kernels would be required.',r'The prime on delta is clearly visible in the scan. Independent Fourier-symbol and circle Fourier-mode checks agree on the order mismatch.')
correction('P001077','Analytic sequence second component',r'\(f_2(z_1,z_1)\).',r'In the symmetric Fock interpretation use \(f_2(z_1,z_2)\).',r'The second subscript resembles 1 in the scan, already flagged by the transcription. The n-variable sequence supplies the intended two independent variables; preserve the diplomatic reading and record this as an interpretive emendation.',typ='ambiguity')
