talk(583,'1983-02-14','V. L. Ginzburg','P001076-P001080','Duistermaat--Heckman localization and orbital integrals',r'The entry occupies P001078 and the upper part of P001079. It presents Hamiltonian torus actions, regular reduction, affine variation of reduced symplectic classes, polynomial densities and exact stationary phase, then relates these to convexity and characters. The 1982 theorem is expository here. The nonisolated fixed-component formula is abbreviated beyond validity: normal-bundle curvature terms are needed. The historical allusions to Semenov-Tian-Shansky and Vergne have identifiable pre-talk sources.')
item(583,'P001078','definition','Hamiltonian torus actions and the moment map',r'A torus acts symplectically, each infinitesimal field has a Hamiltonian, and the Hamiltonians commute; equivalently, give commuting functions with periodic flows. The map is \(\mu(m)(X)=H_X(m)\).',r'Let a torus T act on a compact connected symplectic manifold \((M,\omega)\). A Hamiltonian action has a T-invariant map \(\mu:M\to\mathfrak t^*\) satisfying \(d\langle\mu,X\rangle=\iota_{X_M}\omega\), with the sign convention fixed accordingly. Conversely, commuting complete Hamiltonian flows whose joint period lattice defines T give such an action. Individual periodicity alone does not specify an effective torus of the claimed rank.','E',r'The setting is explicit in \citet[section 6]{atiyahbott1984moment}, explaining the already published 1982 result \citep{duistermaatheckman1982variation}.',['atiyahbott1984moment','duistermaatheckman1982variation'],reconstruction='Connectedness, the period lattice and moment-map sign are supplied; the later paper is exposition of the named 1982 theorem.')
item(583,'P001078','claim','The cocycle behind commuting Hamiltonian fields',r'Commuting fields need not come from Poisson-commuting Hamiltonians; the example uses y and minus x on the plane. The cocycle must be trivial.',r'If Hamiltonian vector fields commute on connected M, their Poisson brackets are constant. These constants form a Lie-algebra 2-cocycle; an equivariant moment map requires its vanishing after the allowed normalization. For an abelian Lie algebra a nonzero such cocycle cannot be removed merely by adding constants. The plane translations illustrate this obstruction, but are not a periodic torus action on compact M.','E',r'This is the standard moment-map equivariance obstruction underlying \citet[section 6]{atiyahbott1984moment}. In the compact torus setting, averaging Hamiltonians gives an invariant moment map when the individual infinitesimal contractions are exact.',['atiyahbott1984moment'],reconstruction='The scope of the noncompact translation example and the distinction between a zero cocycle and an arbitrary cohomologically trivial representative are made explicit.',match='partial')
item(583,'P001078','claim','Orbit tangent spaces are isotropic',r'The local condition says that the fields lie in one Lagrangian subspace at each point.',r'For a Hamiltonian torus action, \(\omega(X_M,Y_M)=0\); hence each tangent space to a torus orbit is isotropic and can be extended to a Lagrangian linear subspace of \(T_mM\). This pointwise extension does not imply a globally defined Lagrangian foliation or polarization.','E',r'The identity follows from the commuting moment-map components in \citet[section 6]{atiyahbott1984moment}.',['atiyahbott1984moment'],reconstruction='The notebook’s expressly local qualification is preserved; no global integrability claim is added.',match='partial')
item(583,'P001078','construction','Regular symplectic reduction by the torus',r'\(M_\xi=\mu^{-1}(\xi)\) has a degenerate form, which becomes nondegenerate on \(M_\xi/T\).',r'If \(\xi\) is a regular value and T acts freely on its level, \(M_\xi^{red}=\mu^{-1}(\xi)/T\) is a smooth symplectic manifold with reduced form \(\omega_\xi\). A locally free action gives the corresponding symplectic orbifold. The characteristic kernel on the level is the orbit tangent distribution. Singular values require a stratified reduction, absent from this elementary statement.','E',r'The regular construction predates the talk \citep{marsdenweinstein1974reduction}; it is recalled explicitly in \citet[introduction and section 2.1]{meinrenkensjamaar1999singular}.',['marsdenweinstein1974reduction','meinrenkensjamaar1999singular'],reconstruction='Regularity, freeness and a separate notation for the reduced space are restored.',match='partial')
item(583,'P001078','theorem','The reduced symplectic class varies affinely',r'\(\omega_\xi\) is piecewise linear in \(\xi\), modulo exact differentials.',r'On a connected chamber of regular values, identify nearby reduced spaces by the regular family. Their de Rham classes vary affinely: \([\omega_\xi]=[\omega_{\xi_0}]+\langle\xi-\xi_0,c\rangle\), where c is the fixed curvature/Chern-class vector with a convention-dependent sign. This is a statement about cohomology under the family identifications, not pointwise linearity of differential forms on unrelated fibers.','E',r'The 1982 theorem is explained in \citet[section 7, pp. 22--23]{atiyahbott1984moment}; original publication \citet{duistermaatheckman1982variation}.',['duistermaatheckman1982variation','atiyahbott1984moment'],reconstruction='Affine variation, chamber restrictions and the identification of cohomology groups are explicit.')
item(583,'P001078','theorem','Piecewise polynomial reduced volume and density',r'The corollary is written \(\operatorname{vol}(M_\xi)\) is piecewise polynomial, although \(M_\xi\) was defined as the unreduced level set.',r'For a \(2n\)-dimensional compact Hamiltonian manifold with effective torus dimension k, the reduced symplectic volume on a regular chamber is a polynomial of degree at most \(n-k\). With compatible Haar and Lebesgue normalizations, it is the density of the pushforward of Liouville measure by the moment map. The symplectic volume here belongs to the reduced \(2(n-k)\)-dimensional space, not the presymplectic level set.','E',r'This is the Duistermaat--Heckman polynomial-density conclusion \citep[section 7]{atiyahbott1984moment}; the original appeared in 1982 \citep{duistermaatheckman1982variation}.',['atiyahbott1984moment','duistermaatheckman1982variation'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The notation is corrected to the quotient already described one line earlier.')
item(583,'P001078','theorem','Exact stationary phase for isolated fixed points',r'The integral \(\int_Me^{iH_X}\,dm\) is exactly calculable by stationary phase.',r'For a compact symplectic manifold with Hamiltonian torus action, choose X so that its zero set consists of isolated nondegenerate fixed points. With Liouville amplitude, the stationary-phase sum is exact: it is a finite sum of \(e^{itH_X(p)}\) divided by the product of the linearized weights evaluated at \(tX\), including the phase and measure-normalization factors. This assertion is special to the Hamiltonian setting and its canonical amplitude.','E',r'\citet[section 7, equation (7.5)]{atiyahbott1984moment} gives the isolated-point formula and identifies it with the 1982 theorem \citep{duistermaatheckman1982variation}.',['atiyahbott1984moment','duistermaatheckman1982variation'],reconstruction='Isolation, nondegeneracy, canonical amplitude and scale factors are specified without pretending the notebook fixes the powers of 2 pi or i.')
item(583,'P001078','theorem','Positive-dimensional fixed components need the equivariant Euler class',r'Equation (5) replaces each fixed component by its volume divided by the product of transverse weights.',r'For a fixed component F with normal bundle \(N_F\), the localization contribution is the top-degree part of \(e^{itH_X(F)}\int_F e^\omega/e_T(N_F)(tX)\), in a consistent equivariant convention. The Euler denominator includes curvature/Chern classes of the normal bundle as well as its weights. Consequently it cannot in general be replaced by \(\operatorname{vol}(F)/\prod\lambda_j(X)\); that simplification is valid for isolated points and in special cases when higher characteristic contributions vanish.','E',r'The full formula is \citet[Theorem (1.5)]{duistermaatheckman1983addendum} and \citet[equation (7.4)]{atiyahbott1984moment}. The Addendum circulated as a Utrecht 1982 preprint, explicitly listed in the latter’s reference 12, and appeared in February 1983.',['duistermaatheckman1983addendum','atiyahbott1984moment'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='This is a mathematical emendation of the handwritten formula, not a translation correction.')
item(583,'P001079','remark','Equivariant localization explains the improvements',r'Atiyah and Bott generalized and improved the result; Michèle Vergne did something else on the subject.',r'The identifiable common framework is localization in equivariant cohomology: restriction to the fixed locus becomes invertible after inverting nonzero weights, and equivariant integration is computed through the inverse normal Euler class. The one-line Vergne remark does not uniquely identify which announcement, preprint or formulation was discussed.','E',r'\citet[section 3, equation (3.8)]{atiyahbott1984moment} supplies the cohomological form. \citet[introduction and Theorem 1.6]{berlinevergne1983zeros} identifies its preceding 1982 C. R. announcement and gives the isolated-zero differential-form formula. Both full publications postdate the talk; the earlier announcement and the notebook’s report establish the prepublication context.',['atiyahbott1984moment','berlinevergne1983zeros'],match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The framework is secure, but no exact title is assigned to the words something else.')
item(583,'P001079','theorem','Convexity of the torus moment image',r'The old Atiyah and Guillemin--Sternberg theorem says that the image of the moment map is convex.',r'For a compact connected symplectic manifold with Hamiltonian torus action, the moment image is the convex hull of the images of fixed components and is a convex polytope; moment-map fibers are connected. Compactness, connectedness and the Hamiltonian hypothesis are essential to this formulation.','E',r'\citet[Theorem 1]{atiyah1982convexity} is an exact primary source. The supplied earlier index already records the theorem at 1980-508.15 and 1981-556.12.',['atiyah1982convexity'],reconstruction='The connected-fiber assertion is supplied from the source to clarify the geometry, rather than attributed to the single notebook sentence.')
item(583,'P001079','method','Quadratic local phase and finite Fourier expansion force exactness',r'The proof uses local quadratic form, rapid decay of the remainder, and piecewise polynomial density. The displayed formula inserts t in the exponent but leaves its denominator unchanged.',r'For isolated fixed points, equivariant Darboux coordinates make the Hamiltonian exactly quadratic and Liouville amplitude constant locally. The pushforward density is compactly supported and piecewise polynomial, so its Fourier transform has a finite exponential Laurent expansion. Comparison with stationary phase gives exactness. Each isolated contribution scales as \(t^{-n}\) on a \(2n\)-manifold; omitting this factor from the displayed t-dependent formula is incorrect.','E',r'This is the proof mechanism explained in \citet[section 7]{atiyahbott1984moment}; \citet[Lemma 2.7, Proposition 2.8 and section 4]{semenovtianshansky1973kirillov} gives the earlier compact-orbit quadratic-phase case.',['atiyahbott1984moment','semenovtianshansky1973kirillov'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The argument here is restricted to isolated fixed points; the positive-dimensional case uses the characteristic-class expansion.')
item(583,'P001079','claim','The compact orbital integral is a corrected character',r'The coadjoint-orbit integral is compared with the Harish-Chandra integral and Weyl’s formula. Kirillov says: this is the character.',r'For a compact connected semisimple group and an integral dominant highest weight \(\lambda\), the local Kirillov character formula relates \(\chi_\lambda(\exp X)\) to the Fourier transform of the canonical measure on the shifted orbit \(\mathcal O_{\lambda+\rho}\), multiplied by the appropriate inverse square root of the exponential-map Jacobian. It is not the uncorrected transform of an arbitrary orbit. With the canonical normalization, the shifted orbit volume is the representation dimension.','E',r'\citet[section 3, Theorem 2, equations (26)--(28)]{kirillov1968characters} gives these shifts and normalization before this talk.',['kirillov1968characters'],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',reconstruction='Integrality, the rho shift, canonical measure and Jacobian factor supply the conditions hidden by the short intervention.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(583,'P001079','remark','The ten-year-old Semenov-Tian-Shansky reference',r'A paper by Semenov-Tian-Shansky with similar content appeared about ten years earlier.',r'For compact semisimple coadjoint orbits, the leading stationary-phase orbital-integral formula is exact. The relevant 1973 paper proves the local symplectic quadratic normal form and compares the result with the compact character formula. It explicitly leaves noncompact regularization outside its treatment.','E',r'The allusion matches \citet[introduction, Lemma 2.7, Proposition 2.8 and section 4]{semenovtianshansky1973kirillov}, published in 1973.',['semenovtianshansky1973kirillov'],reconstruction='The exact title and compact scope are recovered from the primary text; the ten-year estimate is accurate.')
item(583,'P001079','remark','Vergne’s proof for discrete-series orbits',r'Michèle Vergne has a proof for noncompact orbits of the discrete series.',r'For a connected semisimple real group in the discrete-series setting with compact Cartan subgroup, the relevant regular elliptic orbit Fourier transform gives the corresponding discrete-series character in Rossmann’s formula, with the specified invariant measures and distributional interpretation. This does not extend compact stationary phase to arbitrary noncompact orbits without conditions.','E',r'\citet[sections 2--4]{vergne1979rossmann} proves Rossmann’s character formula by the oscillator/intertwining argument; received March 1979, revised July 1979, and published in 1979.',['vergne1979rossmann'],reconstruction='The orbit type, character family and distributional qualification come from the exact named primary paper.',match='partial')
correction('P001078','Polynomial volume corollary',r'\(M_\xi=\mu^{-1}(\xi)\), then \(\operatorname{vol}(M_\xi)\) is piecewise polynomial.',r'The symplectic volume is that of \(\mu^{-1}(\xi)/T\), at regular values, with the reduced form.',r'The level-set definition and later volume notation are both visible in the scan. The intervening line already descends the form to the quotient, which is the intended object.')
correction('P001078','Equation (5), nonisolated fixed components',r'\(\sum_j\operatorname{vol}(M_j)e^{iH_X(M_j)}/\prod_k\lambda_{jk}(X)\).',r'Use the equivariant integral over each fixed component with the inverse Euler class of its normal bundle; normal Chern/curvature terms generally contribute.',r'The scan explicitly includes positive-dimensional fixed components and only their volume. Duistermaat--Heckman’s Addendum, Theorem (1.5), and Atiyah--Bott equation (7.4) show the omitted terms.')
correction('P001079','Stationary-phase display with parameter t',r'The exponent contains \(tH_X\), but the denominator remains \(\prod_k\lambda_{jk}(X)\).',r'For isolated fixed points on a \(2n\)-dimensional manifold the denominator must scale with \(\prod_k\lambda_{jk}(tX)=t^n\prod_k\lambda_{jk}(X)\), with the chosen phase factors.',r'The missing t is confirmed in the scan. Scaling in the quadratic Gaussian integral and the exact localization formula both require the power; positive-dimensional components require the additional characteristic terms.')
