ref('gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular',author="Gel'fand, I. M. and Tsetlin, M. L.",title='Finite-dimensional representations of the group of unimodular matrices',journal='Doklady Akademii Nauk SSSR',year=1950,volume=71,pages='825--828',url='https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~deloera/MISC/LA-BIBLIO/trunk/Gelfand/Gelfand7.pdf',annote='Cumulative key reused. Full English collected-paper reprint opened and section 3, formula (3), p. 655 visually read. Integer interlacing arrays label a basis. Original paper submitted 16 February 1950.')
talk(584,'1983-02-14','A. A. Kirillov','P001078-P001082','The SU(3) orbit hexagon and weight multiplicities',r'The intervention begins in the lower part of P001079 and ends with a single request on P001080. It revisits the six-dimensional regular orbit, its torus-moment hexagon and changes in the fibers. The written map uses two conjugacy invariants and therefore cannot produce that hexagon; diagonal projection is the required interpretation. The final weight-multiplicity request recurs from 1981-556 and retains GP-1981-41.')
item(584,'P001079','construction','Regular SU(3) orbits of Hermitian matrices',r'Trace-zero Hermitian 3 by 3 matrices form an eight-dimensional space; an orbit has two invariants, \(\operatorname{tr}A^2\) and \(\operatorname{tr}A^3\), and dimension six.',r'The real vector space of trace-zero Hermitian 3 by 3 matrices has dimension eight. For three distinct eigenvalues with sum zero, the conjugation orbit is \(SU(3)/T^2\), hence has real dimension six. The two indicated trace invariants determine the characteristic polynomial and thus the orbit. Repeated eigenvalues give lower-dimensional orbits.','E',r'This is the coadjoint-orbit realization underlying the Schur--Horn example in \citet[introduction]{atiyah1982convexity}; it already occurs as 1981-556.3 in the earlier index.',['atiyah1982convexity'],reconstruction='Distinct eigenvalues are the missing hypothesis for dimension six.',match='partial')
item(584,'P001079','claim','The hexagon map is diagonal projection, not the invariant map',r'The diagram maps A to \((\operatorname{tr}A^2,\operatorname{tr}A^3)\) and says the image of its orbit is a hexagon.',r'Both trace polynomials are constant on a conjugation orbit, so the displayed map has a one-point image. The torus moment map is instead \(A\mapsto(A_{11},A_{22},A_{33})\) in the plane \(x_1+x_2+x_3=0\). Its image is the convex hull of the permutations of the eigenvalue triple.','E',r'\citet[introduction, Schur--Horn example]{atiyah1982convexity} identifies diagonal projection and its convex hull. Direct conjugation also independently checks that the trace invariants cannot vary.',['atiyah1982convexity'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The diagonal map is a justified emendation needed to recover the drawn hexagon; the literal invariant map is preserved in the ledger.')
item(584,'P001079','theorem','Geometry of the SU(3) permutahedral hexagon',r'The image is almost a regular hexagon: all angles are 120 degrees, and alternate sides have equal lengths.',r'For \(\lambda_1>\lambda_2>\lambda_3\) with sum zero, the convex hull of the six permutations of \(\lambda\) is an equiangular hexagon in the trace-zero plane. The two alternating side lengths are \(\sqrt2(\lambda_1-\lambda_2)\) and \(\sqrt2(\lambda_2-\lambda_3)\); it is regular exactly when these differences agree. Repeated eigenvalues collapse it to a triangle or a point.','E',r'The convex-hull description is the classical matrix example in \citet[introduction]{atiyah1982convexity}. The angle and length statements follow by subtracting adjacent permuted vertices. Earlier occurrence: 1981-556.3.',['atiyah1982convexity'],reconstruction='The eigenvalue inequalities and Euclidean metric on the trace-zero plane specify the notebook’s almost regular description.')
item(584,'P001079','remark','Changing fibers and piecewise linear reduced volume',r'The fiber structure changes, the singularities are known, and the notes ask: polynomials?',r'On a regular six-dimensional orbit, a regular value of the rank-two moment map has a four-dimensional level set; its torus reduction has real dimension two. The reduced symplectic area, equivalently the normalized Duistermaat--Heckman density, is piecewise affine on regular chambers. The handwritten crossing lines do not uniquely label all singular strata, so no complete fiber classification is recovered from the sketch alone.','E',r'The degree bound follows from the 1982 polynomial-density theorem \citep[section 7]{atiyahbott1984moment}. The same level/reduced dimension distinction occurs at 1981-556.4 in the earlier index.',['atiyahbott1984moment'],match='partial',reconstruction='Only the dimension and density consequences fixed by the regular-value hypotheses are asserted; labels absent from the drawing are not invented.')
pid=item(584,'P001080','problem','Recovering weight multiplicities geometrically',r'\ru{По выписанным выше формулам вычислить кратности веса}: calculate weight multiplicities from the preceding formulas.',r'For an integral dominant \(SU(3)\) highest weight, recover each torus-weight multiplicity from the corresponding orbit/character data. Exact multiplicities require the integral lattice, character normalization and its shifts, or an equivariant quantization with the correctly shifted reduced line bundle; classical reduced area by itself is not the finite-dimensional multiplicity.','E',r'Exact finite-dimensional multiplicities were already computable from the interlacing basis of \citet[section 3, formula (3)]{gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular} and the shifted orbital character formula \citep[section 3]{kirillov1968characters}. The geometric reduced-index formulation, including singular quotients, is supplied by \citet[Theorem 2.9 and Corollary 2.11]{meinrenkensjamaar1999singular}, first circulated July 1997, published 1999. This repeats 1981-556.1 and 1981-556.6.',['gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular','kirillov1968characters','meinrenkensjamaar1999singular'],problem='GP-1981-41',reconstruction='An integral highest weight and the distinction between classical density and quantum index are required to make the one-line request precise.',status='compact integral-orbit weight multiplicities are established; the later reduced-index theorem explains the geometric interpretation, including singular levels.')
P.append(dict(id='GP-1981-41',title='Geometric realization of compact-group weight multiplicities',statement=r'For an integral compact coadjoint orbit quantizing an irreducible representation, express its torus-weight multiplicities geometrically through the correctly normalized orbital character or the index of the corresponding reduced space, with the shifted quotient line bundle.',occurrences=['1981-556.1','1981-556.6',pid],status=r'As of November 2026: finite-dimensional multiplicities are classical; the reduced-space index interpretation extends to singular reductions by \citet[Theorem 2.9 and Corollary 2.11]{meinrenkensjamaar1999singular} (July 1997 preprint; 1999 publication). A level set is not itself the reduced space, and reduced volume alone is not the finite-dimensional multiplicity.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular','kirillov1968characters','meinrenkensjamaar1999singular']))
correction('P001079','SU(3) orbit projection diagram',r'\(A\mapsto(\operatorname{tr}A^2,\operatorname{tr}A^3)\) on an orbit has hexagonal image.',r'These invariants are constant on the orbit. The hexagonal moment image belongs to the diagonal projection \(A\mapsto(A_{11},A_{22},A_{33})\), with sum zero.',r'The two trace expressions are visibly written in the scanned diagram. Conjugacy invariance proves its image is a point; the preceding orbit discussion and Schur--Horn convex hull identify the intended diagonal moment map.')
