ref('arnold1983mathieu',author="Arnol'd, V. I.",title='Remarks on the perturbation theory for problems of {Mathieu} type',journal='Russian Mathematical Surveys',year=1983,volume=38,number=4,pages='215--233',doi='10.1070/RM1983v038n04ABEH004210',url='https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/rm2950',annote='Full English primary PDF opened and sections 1--5, Theorems 1--7, Catalan footnote and Supplement of 30 March 1983 read. Received 1 February 1983; the notebook talk is 21 February; publication is later in 1983. The Supplement explicitly acknowledges Bloch 1958, Levy--Keller 1963 and Hochstadt 1964 as antecedents. Receipt is not treated as proof of public circulation.')
ref('bloch1958perturbations',author='Bloch, Claude',title='Sur la théorie des perturbations des états liés',journal='Nuclear Physics',year=1958,volume=6,pages='329--347',doi='10.1016/0029-5582(58)90116-0',url='https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9780444601148_A23669232/preview-9780444601148_A23669232.pdf',annote='Publisher preview of collected Scientific Works opened: contents p. xvii confirms original article and pp. 329--347. Article abstract retrieved in publisher search, but direct ScienceDirect opening returned 403. The theorem-level antecedent is explicitly attributed by Arnold’s 30 March 1983 Supplement; full Bloch article not read.')
ref('levykeller1963instability',author='Levy, Dorothy M. and Keller, Joseph B.',title='Instability intervals of {Hill}’s equation',journal='Communications on Pure and Applied Mathematics',year=1963,volume=16,number=4,pages='469--476',doi='10.1002/cpa.3160160406',url='https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/cpa.3160160406',annote='Publisher metadata opened; confirms November 1963 publication. Full PDF request returned the abstract/metadata page. The comparison with Arnold’s Theorem 3 is read in Arnold’s explicit 30 March 1983 Supplement, not independently verified in the 1963 full text.')
ref('hochstadt1964instability',author='Hochstadt, Harry',title='Instability intervals of {Hill}’s equation',journal='Communications on Pure and Applied Mathematics',year=1964,volume=17,number=2,pages='251--255',doi='10.1002/cpa.3160170209',url='https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/cpa.3160170209',annote='Publisher metadata opened; confirms May 1964 publication. Full PDF request returned the abstract/metadata page. The relation to Arnold’s Theorem 7 is explicit in Arnold’s 30 March 1983 Supplement; original full proof not independently read.')
ref('grebertkappelerposchel2004gaps',author='Grébert, Benoît and Kappeler, Thomas and Pöschel, Jürgen',title='A note on gaps of {Hill}’s equation',journal='International Mathematics Research Notices',year=2004,volume=2004,number=50,pages='2703--2717',doi='10.1155/S1073792804132807',url='https://www.math.sciences.univ-nantes.fr/~grebert/GKP-IMRN.pdf',annote='Cumulative key reused. Primary author PDF reopened; Theorems 1.1--1.2 and spectral background read. It gives regularity and finite-gap density, not a unique solution to Gelfand’s unspecified one-gap perturbation question.')
ref('poschel2004weighted','misc',author='Pöschel, Jürgen',title='Hill’s potentials in weighted {Sobolev} spaces and their spectral gaps',year=2004,howpublished='Stuttgarter Mathematische Berichte 2004/12; later arXiv:0908.0491',url='https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0491',annote='Full primary preprint opened; section 1, Theorems 1 and 3, smooth/analytic equivalences and historical references read. Title page says version 4.3 August 2004; arXiv upload 4 August 2009 is not the first circulation. Used for precise regularity qualification, not a new 1983 theorem.')
ref('krichever1983hessians',author='Krichever, I. M.',title='``Hessians\'\' of integrals of the {Korteweg--de Vries} equation and perturbations of finite-zone solutions',journal='Doklady Akademii Nauk SSSR',year=1983,volume=270,number=6,pages='1312--1317',url='https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/dan46140',annote='Metadata and full Russian primary PDF opened. Theorems 1--3, Lemma 3 and equations (6), (13)--(19) read. Presented 19 October 1982; received 23 November 1982; published 1983. Presentation and receipt are not treated as proof of public circulation. This source supplies a thematic forward link to record 586, not an exact identification of Gelfand’s unspecified question.')
talk(585,'1983-02-21','V. I. Arnold','P001080-P001088','Mathieu gaps, Catalan diagrams and spectral perturbation',r'The actual talk is P001081--P001086; P001080 belongs to Kirillov and P001087 begins Krichever. Arnold explains delayed splitting of Hill eigenvalues for trigonometric-polynomial perturbations by quiver paths, Catalan bracket fillings and a reversal involution. The paper received on 1 February 1983 and published later that year closely matches the talk. Its 30 March supplement acknowledges older results of Bloch, Levy--Keller and Hochstadt, so the estimates and coefficient identities are not all classified as new 1983 discoveries. Gelfand’s one-gap question remains underdetermined in this record.')
item(585,'P001081','construction','Instability tongues and periodic or antiperiodic spectra',r'\(\ddot x+(\omega^2+\varepsilon a(t))x=0\), \(a(t+2\pi)=a(t)\); the drawing shows tongues at \(\omega=1/2,1,3/2,\ldots\).',r'For real periodic a, the monodromy has determinant one. Instability occurs when one Floquet multiplier has modulus greater than one; the other then has modulus less than one. Tongue boundaries are periodic or antiperiodic eigenvalue loci. For the unperturbed equation their positive-frequency tips are \(\omega=N/2\), \(N\ge1\).','E',r'The classical spectral interpretation is recalled in \citet[section 3, proof of Theorem 3]{arnold1983mathieu}.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='Reality and the monodromy criterion are supplied; the diagram’s half-integer labeling is retained.')
item(585,'P001081','claim','Generic first-order opening of Hill gaps',r'For a coefficient a in general position the boundaries have first-order contact everywhere.',r'Fix \(N\ge1\). For a real bounded \(2\pi\)-periodic perturbation with nonzero Fourier coefficient \(a_N\), degenerate perturbation theory on \(\operatorname{span}(e^{iNt/2},e^{-iNt/2})\) gives a spectral gap \(2|\varepsilon a_N|+O(\varepsilon^2)\) in the eigenvalue variable. Thus a coefficient with every \(a_N\ne0\) has first-order opening at every fixed positive tip. This is not a uniform-in-N remainder statement.','E',r'The contrast between generic Hill equations and polynomial perturbations is explicit in \citet[introduction and section 3]{arnold1983mathieu}; the first coefficient is the restriction of A to the double eigenspace.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The nonvanishing Fourier coefficient and the chosen eigenvalue variable make the genericity statement precise.',match='partial')
item(585,'P001081','claim','Exponential gap decay requires analytic regularity',r'The generic Hill statement is followed by: the width decreases exponentially.',r'For a real smooth periodic potential, high-energy gap lengths decay faster than any inverse power of their index. Exponential decay in the index is the analytic-potential conclusion and is not guaranteed by smoothness or periodicity alone. This concerns the high-index limit at a fixed potential, distinct from the small-amplitude limit at a fixed gap.','E',r'\citet[section 1, historical equivalences and Theorems 1, 3]{poschel2004weighted} distinguishes the smooth and analytic cases and credits the pre-talk Hochstadt and Trubowitz results. Its 2004 preprint is used for verification, not as a claim of first discovery.',['poschel2004weighted'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The missing regularity and limiting regime are restored; the notebook does not state analyticity.',match='partial')
item(585,'P001081','claim','Mathieu’s cosine perturbation has increasingly high tangency',r'For \(a(t)=\cos t\), successive zone boundaries have increasing orders of contact; this is the degree-one example.',r'For the real Mathieu equation and each fixed \(N\ge1\), the Nth gap opens at order \(|\varepsilon|^N\), with a nonzero coefficient. The claim concerns small amplitude at fixed N and the standard positive-frequency branch; it should not be confused with a uniform estimate over all gaps.','E',r'The classical Mathieu behavior is recalled in \citet[introduction]{arnold1983mathieu}; the paper’s supplement identifies the earlier Hill-gap literature \citep{levykeller1963instability,hochstadt1964instability}.',['arnold1983mathieu','levykeller1963instability','hochstadt1964instability'],reconstruction='The fixed-index asymptotic and nonzero cosine coefficient are made explicit.',match='partial')
item(585,'P001081-P001085','theorem','The polynomial-degree gap bound uses a ceiling and an upper estimate',r'Equation (3) writes the width as \(\asymp\varepsilon^{[N/p]}\) when \(\deg a=p\). The later proof only delays splitting.',r'For a fixed \(N\ge1\) and a trigonometric polynomial a of degree \(p\ge1\), the two periodic or antiperiodic eigenvalues issuing from the \(N/2\) double frequency satisfy \(|\lambda_N^+-\lambda_N^-|=O(|\varepsilon|^{\lceil N/p\rceil})\). For real a this bounds the Nth instability gap. A universal matching lower bound is false: coefficients can cancel and gaps can remain closed. The ceiling is \(-\lfloor-N/p\rfloor\), not \(\lfloor N/p\rfloor\).','E',r'The precise statements are \citet[Theorems 3 and 7]{arnold1983mathieu}. His Supplement dated 30 March 1983 credits \citet{levykeller1963instability} for the even case and \citet{hochstadt1964instability} for the general case. The original antecedent papers were metadata-checked; their theorem comparison is Arnold’s explicit report.',['arnold1983mathieu','levykeller1963instability','hochstadt1964instability'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The bracket notation is unexplained in the notebook; the paper fixes the ceiling. Independently, the asymptotic equivalence is too strong even if the brackets meant ceiling.',status='the qualified gap bound is established and predates the talk; Arnold’s proof and its exact match to the notebook are verified.')
item(585,'P001081','question','Perturbing a one-gap potential',r'Gelfand observes that the constant potential is zero-gap and asks what happens near a one-gap potential.',r'The question suggests replacing the constant background by a real periodic one-gap potential and studying perturbed spectral gaps. It does not specify the perturbation class, which spectral gaps, a norm, or whether the target is a first variation, a uniform asymptotic, or persistence of finite-gap structure. These lead to distinct problems.','F',r'The next meeting, items 1982-586.3--6, supplies a thematic continuation: \citet[Theorems 1--3, Lemma 3 and equations (6), (13)--(19)]{krichever1983hessians} gives a precise first-order finite-gap perturbation framework, including fixed-period constraints, Hessian diagonalization and gap-opening modes. The paper was presented on 19 October 1982, received on 23 November 1982 and published in 1983; those administrative dates do not establish public circulation. The spectral framework and finite-gap density in \citet[Theorems 1.1--1.2]{grebertkappelerposchel2004gaps} are another related result. Neither publication resolves all possible readings of the notebook’s sentence. Current primary-source searches did not identify the missing formulation.',['krichever1983hessians','grebertkappelerposchel2004gaps'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='the historical question needs a specified perturbation and asymptotic regime before it can be designated solved or open; the link to the following meeting is thematic and does not establish an identical problem.')
item(585,'P001081','method','Rayleigh--Schrödinger expansion at a simple eigenvalue',r'Equation (4) expands the eigenvector and eigenvalue in powers of epsilon for \(\Lambda+\varepsilon A\).',r'For a finite-dimensional diagonalizable operator with a simple eigenvalue \(\lambda_0\), analytic perturbation gives a normalized local eigenvector and eigenvalue power series. Equating coefficients recursively computes them after fixing the component of the eigenvector in the original eigenspace. A formal expansion can also be considered without claiming convergence.','E',r'\citet[section 1]{arnold1983mathieu} starts from this classical expansion and later acknowledges \citet{bloch1958perturbations} for general diagrammatic coefficient formulas.',['arnold1983mathieu','bloch1958perturbations'],reconstruction='Simplicity, normalization and finite-dimensional analytic validity are supplied.')
item(585,'P001082','definition','Catalan closed form and the erroneous seventh entry',r'The sequence is \(1,1,2,5,14,42,138\), and \(a_n=(2n)!/(n!(n+1)!)\).',r'With indexing \(C_0=1\), the Catalan numbers are \(C_n=(2n)!/(n!(n+1)!)\); thus \(C_6=132\), not 138. The closed form in the notes agrees with the corrected sequence.','E',r'\citet[section 4, Catalan remark]{arnold1983mathieu} prints 132 and the same closed formula. The value was independently checked from both the factorial formula and convolution recurrence.',['arnold1983mathieu'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The starting index is specified to align the two initial ones.')
item(585,'P001082','claim','The Catalan convolution has incorrect bounds',r'Equation (5) is \(a_{n+1}=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}a_ja_{n-j}\).',r'For the stated indexing, \(C_{n+1}=\sum_{j=0}^{n}C_jC_{n-j}\). The omitted endpoint terms are necessary. The generating series \(C(z)=\sum_{n\ge0}C_nz^n\) satisfies \(C=1+zC^2\), with the branch determined by \(C(0)=1\).','E',r'The Catalan objects in \citet[section 4]{arnold1983mathieu} obey this standard recurrence. The notebook’s printed bounds already fail at n equal to one.',['arnold1983mathieu'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The generating equation is the explicit version of the notebook’s generating-function instruction.',match='partial')
item(585,'P001082','claim','The polygon interpretation has an index shift',r'The number \(a_n=(2n)!/(n!(n+1)!)\) is called the number of simplicial subdivisions of an n-gon.',r'The number \(C_n\) counts triangulations of a convex \((n+2)\)-gon by noncrossing diagonals, with no added vertices. A convex n-gon has \(C_{n-2}\) such triangulations. Allowing arbitrary additional vertices would not give this finite count.','E',r'\citet[section 4, Catalan remark]{arnold1983mathieu} explicitly prints the \((n+2)\)-gon.',['arnold1983mathieu'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='Convexity, triangulation and the prohibition on new vertices are restored.')
item(585,'P001082','remark','Dimension of curve moduli',r'Riemann surfaces of genus g have \(3g-3\) moduli; branch points on the sphere are invoked.',r'For smooth compact complex curves of genus \(g\ge2\), the moduli space has complex dimension \(3g-3\). The formula requires separate treatment in genera zero and one. In the subsequent genus-\(2k\) covering example, \(6k\) simple branch points modulo target projective transformations give dimension \(6k-3=3g-3\).','E',r'The specific covering example is identified by \citet[section 4, footnote]{arnold1983mathieu}; the dimension comparison follows from Riemann--Hurwitz and the target automorphism quotient.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='Genus restriction and dimension over the complex numbers are editorial specifications; the branch count belongs to the following special example.',match='partial')
item(585,'P001082','theorem','Catalan many minimal pencils on a general even-genus curve',r'Griffiths and Harris count ways of obtaining one and the same surface as a covering; the answer is the Catalan sequence.',r'A general smooth complex curve of genus \(2k\), \(k\ge1\), has \(C_k\) pencils of degree \(k+1\), equivalently maps to the projective line modulo target automorphisms, with \(6k\) simple branch points in the generic case. Special curves need not have this finite count.','E',r'\citet[section 4, footnote]{arnold1983mathieu} states exactly this example and identifies Griffiths--Harris, Principles of Algebraic Geometry (1978), as the antecedent. The book’s result was not independently opened, so this historical attribution is indirect through Arnold’s primary paper.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='Even genus, general curve, degree and equivalence under target automorphisms are recovered from the footnote rather than the abbreviated notebook.',verification='indirect',match='partial')
item(585,'P001082','definition','Bracket symbols as Catalan objects',r'A properly formed string of letters and parentheses has length equal to letters plus opening parentheses; the number of shapes is Catalan.',r'Consider balanced parenthesis words with nonempty contents in each pair, and letters serving as indistinguishable ordered slots. Give each letter and each opening parenthesis weight one. The shapes of length n are counted by \(C_n\); length two gives \(ab,(a)\), and length three gives five shapes. Letters become independently chosen quiver vertices only in a later filling.','A',r'This exact notation and its counting appear in \citet[section 4]{arnold1983mathieu}, received 1 February and published later in 1983. The Supplement identifies the equivalent Catalan staircase diagrams in Bloch’s 1958 approach; A marks this particular presentation, not priority for Catalan perturbation theory.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='Distinguishing abstract shapes from fillings prevents counting infinitely many alphabet choices.')
item(585,'P001082-P001083','construction','The spectral quiver and its chronological products',r'The eigenspaces \(V_i\) are vertices and \(A_{ij}:V_i\to V_j\) are arrows. Products are taken following the arrows.',r'Let \(\Lambda\) act as the distinct scalar \(\lambda_i\) on each block \(V_i\). Draw an arrow \(i\to j\) when the perturbing block \(A_{ij}\) is nonzero. For a path \(i_0\to i_1\to\cdots\to i_k\), its chronological product is \(A_{i_{k-1}i_k}\cdots A_{i_0i_1}:V_{i_0}\to V_{i_k}\), with composition read right to left.','A',r'\citet[section 1, definitions]{arnold1983mathieu} gives this exact quiver presentation. Diagrammatic perturbation itself is older, as the paper’s Supplement explicitly acknowledges.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The direction convention is fixed from the handwritten map, so no transposition of the block indices is silently introduced.')
item(585,'P001083','claim','Local finiteness controls formal coefficients, not convergence by itself',r'Everything is assumed finite or locally finite; for Mathieu-type operators the quiver is locally finite, and the perturbed subspace is called analytic.',r'A locally finite quiver has finitely many paths of any fixed length issuing from a fixed vertex, so the prescribed perturbation coefficient is a finite sum. This is a formal statement. In finite dimensions a spectrally isolated cluster has an analytic spectral projection; for Hill operators bounded polynomial perturbations and the elliptic unperturbed operator give analytic cluster perturbation. Local finiteness alone does not prove convergence for an arbitrary infinite operator.','E',r'\citet[section 1, Remark 2]{arnold1983mathieu} explicitly makes this distinction: it treats formal series or finite matrices, and invokes ellipticity for Mathieu convergence.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The analytic claim is confined to a specified operator setting, not inferred from the graph alone.',match='partial')
item(585,'P001083','construction','The perturbed invariant subspace as a graph',r'\(V_0(\varepsilon)\) is the graph of \(B(\varepsilon):V_0\to\bigoplus_{i\ne0}V_i\), and equation (7) imposes invariance.',r'For a finite-dimensional diagonalizable \(\Lambda\) and an isolated eigenvalue \(\lambda_0\), the small perturbed spectral subspace is the graph of an analytic \(B(\varepsilon)\) with \(B(0)=0\), relative to the fixed spectral complement. The invariance equation is \((\Lambda+\varepsilon A)(\iota+B)=(\iota+B)S(\varepsilon)\), where \(\iota:V_0\hookrightarrow V\). This notation avoids treating a projection and an inclusion as the same typed map.','E',r'\citet[section 2]{arnold1983mathieu} writes the graph equation. The general perturbation method predates the talk; the Supplement credits \citet{bloch1958perturbations}.',['arnold1983mathieu','bloch1958perturbations'],reconstruction='Isolation, fixed complement and the inclusion convention are supplied.')
item(585,'P001083','theorem','The secular operator preserves the cluster spectrum',r'The operator \(\lambda_0+\varepsilon\alpha^1+\cdots\) is called secular; restriction to \(V_0(\varepsilon)\) is similar to it.',r'The effective operator \(S(\varepsilon)=(\iota+B)^{-1}(\Lambda+\varepsilon A)|_{\operatorname{graph}B}(\iota+B)\) acts on the fixed space \(V_0\) and has exactly the perturbed cluster eigenvalues with algebraic multiplicity. Analyticity of the matrix S does not by itself imply analyticity of each individual eigenvalue for a general non-self-adjoint family.','E',r'The similarity and spectrum assertion is explicit in \citet[section 2]{arnold1983mathieu}; the exact notebook formulation matches it.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The inverse is restricted to the graph and multiplicity is specified; no unsupported eigenvalue-analyticity claim is added.')
item(585,'P001083','theorem','Closed paths give universal coefficient formulas',r'\(\alpha_k=\sum_{|\gamma|=k}\Pi_\gamma(A)Q_\gamma(\Lambda)\), where the loops start and end at zero and Q is rational in eigenvalues.',r'In the graph-normalized effective operator, the coefficient of \(\varepsilon^k\) is a sum over length-k closed paths at the chosen eigenspace. The factor \(\Pi_\gamma(A)\) is the ordered block product, and \(Q_\gamma\) is a universal rational function of the eigenvalue differences. If several parenthesis fillings yield the same loop, their denominator contributions must be added in \(Q_\gamma\).','E',r'This is \citet[Theorems 1--2 and section 4, Remark 1]{arnold1983mathieu}. His dated Supplement explicitly credits Bloch’s 1958 antecedent for these coefficient results \citep{bloch1958perturbations}; Arnold’s proof was read directly.',['arnold1983mathieu','bloch1958perturbations'],reconstruction='Graph normalization and the aggregation of different fillings specify the otherwise ambiguous rational coefficient.')
item(585,'P001083-P001084','construction','Filled bracket diagrams remember the denominator data',r'Insert vertices into a bracket symbol; opening parentheses become zeros, closing parentheses are omitted from the solid path, and dashed arrows are then added. Different fillings can have one loop.',r'Fill each letter slot by a nonzero quiver vertex. Put zero at both ends and at every opening parenthesis to form a solid path; require its arrows to be allowed. Add a dashed arrow from the vertex immediately preceding each closing parenthesis to the zero of its matching opening parenthesis. A symbol of length \(k-1\) yields a loop of k solid arrows. Dashed arrows encode information lost by the solid loop alone.','A',r'The definitions and examples are an exact match to \citet[section 4]{arnold1983mathieu}. This is Arnold’s particular diagrammatic construction; the underlying coefficient expansion is older.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='Admissibility and the exclusion of zero from letter slots are restored from the published definitions.')
item(585,'P001084','theorem','The denominator rule for each filled diagram',r'Equation (11) sums \(\Pi_{\gamma(I)}(A)/q_I(\Lambda)\); equation (12) multiplies \(\lambda_0-\lambda_i\) for solid arrows into nonzero i and \(\lambda_j-\lambda_0\) for dashed arrows from nonzero j.',r'For each admissible filling I of length \(k-1\), take exactly the indicated signed product \(q_I\), counting arrows with multiplicity. Its contribution to \(\alpha_k\) is the chronological solid-loop product divided by \(q_I\). Summing over all fillings gives the coefficient. In particular the filling \((i)j\) has denominator \((\lambda_0-\lambda_i)(\lambda_0-\lambda_j)(\lambda_i-\lambda_0)\), agreeing with the notebook example.','E',r'This is \citet[Theorem 4 and the denominator definition]{arnold1983mathieu}. The Supplement credits the equivalent coefficient theorem to Bloch 1958, while recording Arnold’s distinct parenthesis notation \citep{bloch1958perturbations}.',['arnold1983mathieu','bloch1958perturbations'],reconstruction='The exact sign and multiplicities are retained; no denominator factor is changed.')
item(585,'P001085','construction','Fourier blocks of a trigonometric perturbation',r'\(V_k=\langle e_k,e_{-k}\rangle\), \(\Lambda=d^2/dt^2\), and equation (16) gives a two by two matrix marked with a question mark.',r'For \(i,j>0\) and \(A_{ij}:V_i\to V_j\), in the ordered bases \((e_i,e_{-i})\), \((e_j,e_{-j})\), multiplication by \(a(t)=\sum a_se^{ist}\) has block \(\begin{pmatrix}a_{j-i}&a_{i+j}\\a_{-i-j}&a_{i-j}\end{pmatrix}\). The printed signs are correct for that convention. The zero-frequency block has dimension one; odd-numbered tongues instead use the half-integer Fourier lattice.','E',r'\citet[section 3, Lemma 1 and preceding setup]{arnold1983mathieu} gives the same matrix and treats both integer and half-integer frequencies.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The zero block and antiperiodic sector are specified; the handwritten question mark does not require a false correction.')
item(585,'P001085','claim','Remote blocks are scalar for an even polynomial',r'If \(i+j>p\), off-diagonal entries vanish; for \(a_{-s}=a_s\), the block is scalar. Products of scalar blocks remain scalar.',r'If a has Fourier support \(|s|\le p\) and \(i+j>p\), the off-diagonal block entries vanish. If a is even, the two remaining diagonal entries coincide, so \(A_{ij}=a_{j-i}I\). For a general non-even polynomial the remote block is only diagonal; equality of the summed effective diagonal entries needs the later involution.','E',r'This is \citet[section 3, Lemma 2 and Remark 2]{arnold1983mathieu}; it is the elementary ingredient of the older even-gap estimate.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The distinction between evenness and reality is explicit: an even polynomial may have complex coefficients.')
item(585,'P001085','method','Reflection of a short loop delays splitting',r'The combinatorial lemma says \(\tau p<2k\) implies \(\alpha_\tau\) is scalar. A loop is reflected across zero at a nonremote arrow.',r'A loop based at k on the nonnegative frequency lattice, with \(\tau\) arrows each of length at most p, cannot contain an arrow \(i\to j\) with \(i+j\le p\) when \(\tau p<2k\): reflecting the subsequent path would give a path from k to minus k of total length at most \(\tau p\). Hence all factors are remote. For even a, this makes \(\alpha_\tau\) scalar and prevents splitting to that order.','A',r'The proof is exactly \citet[section 3, Lemma 3 and proof of Theorem 3]{arnold1983mathieu}. A labels this short combinatorial proof; the resulting spectral bound itself is E because of Levy--Keller’s 1963 result.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The reflected path is a length argument; the notebook’s parenthetical homotopy language is not needed for a stronger topological theorem.')
item(585,'P001086','method','Dual bracket fillings equalize the diagonal corrections',r'Opening parentheses split a word into fragments; reverse letters within each fragment with their attached closing parentheses, then restore the opening parentheses. The contributions exchange the diagonal entries.',r'In the remote-loop regime, this operation is an involution on admissible fillings. Reversed arrows interchange the diagonal entries of the commuting Fourier blocks, while the signed denominator remains unchanged because the letter multiset and its closing-parenthesis multiplicities are preserved. Pairing the contributions therefore makes every effective coefficient below the first allowed splitting order scalar, even for a non-even complex trigonometric polynomial.','A',r'\citet[section 5, Lemmas 2--4 and proof of Theorem 7]{arnold1983mathieu} matches the final page. The Supplement attributes the spectral estimate to Hochstadt 1964; A marks the verified involution proof, not a new first proof of the estimate.',['arnold1983mathieu'],reconstruction='The remote-loop restriction and denominator invariance are supplied from the proof. Complex coefficients are allowed, so no Hermitian or positivity assertion is inferred.')
correction('P001081','Equation (3), order of polynomial gap opening',r'Width \(\asymp\varepsilon^{[N/p]}\).',r'The generally valid conclusion is \(O(|\varepsilon|^{\lceil N/p\rceil})\); a matching lower bound requires additional nonvanishing conditions.',r'The scan confirms the two-sided asymptotic symbol and the unexplained brackets. Arnold’s Theorems 3 and 7 explicitly use an upper bound and minus the floor of minus N/p. This separates an ambiguous bracket convention from the false universal asymptotic equivalence.')
correction('P001081','Generic Hill high-index width','The width decreases exponentially for the unspecified generic periodic coefficient.','Exponential high-index decay requires analytic regularity; smooth real periodic potentials generally give rapid polynomial decay instead.',r'The scan and Russian text say exp decreases without an analytic hypothesis. Pöschel’s section 1 distinguishes the smooth and analytic spectral equivalences; the small-amplitude and high-index limits also need separation.')
correction('P001082','Catalan sequence final number','138','132',r'The scan clearly writes 138. The adjacent factorial formula and the convolution recurrence independently give 132, as printed in Arnold’s section 4.')
correction('P001082','Equation (5), Catalan recurrence',r'\(a_{n+1}=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}a_ja_{n-j}\).',r'With \(a_n=C_n\), use \(C_{n+1}=\sum_{j=0}^{n}C_jC_{n-j}\).',r'The scan clearly shows the upper bound n minus one and lower bound one. The listed initial values force the endpoint terms; at n equal to one the written empty sum contradicts \(C_2=2\).')
correction('P001082','Triangulation interpretation','The nth Catalan number counts simplicial subdivisions of an n-gon.','It counts triangulations of a convex (n+2)-gon by noncrossing diagonals, with no new vertices.',r'The scan writes n-gon. Arnold’s section 4 explicitly writes (n+2)-gon, and the triangle/quadrilateral counts independently fix the shift.')
